        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nWe need for proposal P1267.\n\nText: Embed Auto-Logging Into Every System Architecture. We build standardized logging middleware layer into each high-risk AI system's codebase so events captured automatically... covers use-period timestamps... engineering teams adopt mandatory logging SDK at design stage... integration tests verifying log completeness before internal validation. Dedicated architecture review gate checks compliance with Article 12(1)-(3) before release. Without native logging built in, retrofitting is prohibitively expensive and market reputation suffers...\n\nRate each subscale.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws...\n\nEvidence: building logging middleware to comply with EU AI Act is standard compliance, no violation. So acceptability 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEmbedding SDK at design stage requires engineering changes across pipelines, integration tests, architecture review gate. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivial low cost? Might be significant change? The proposal says retrofitting prohibitively expensive, so building in is better. Implementability likely 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2? Standard logging middleware is common. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal directly builds auto-logging per Article 12(1)-(3). So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nIt captures events automatically, timestamps, reference db, etc. Seems would solve logging mandate. So effectiveness 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding auto-logging middleware is common practice for compliance. Mundane. So originality 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is standard compliance logging practice; architecture review gate is standard. Probably paradigm preserving =1. Could be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces middleware layer, mandatory SDK at design stage. That's still treating compliance as logging practice. Might be 2? The definition: slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's exactly this. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says embed auto-logging into every system architecture, standardized middleware. That is new element but still compliance as logging. So maybe 2.\n\nLet's decide: Paradigm preserving is standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. But they are changing design stage to include logging SDK, review gate. That's a change. Could be slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nI think rating 2 for paradigm_relatedness.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOrder listed: acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nEnsure integer level.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably plain text. The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nProbably output as lines:\n\nid: P1267\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences concise.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Embedding automated logs to meet Article 12 is legally required and socially expected, violating no norms. -> score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: It requires a mandatory SDK, integration tests and an architecture gate across all pipelines, which is reasonably feasible but involves organizational change. -> score 3\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly builds automatic event logging for use periods, reference databases, matches and verifier identities mandated by Article 12(1)-(3). -> score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, native auto-logging would provide complete traceability throughout system lifetime and satisfy the stated mandate. -> score 4\n\nOriginality evidence: Standardized logging middleware and design-stage SDKs are common, mundane compliance engineering practices. -> score 1\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a middleware tool and review gate but still treats compliance as conventional logging practice without changing R&D-compliance relationship. -> score 2\n\nProvide.\n\n",
