'll go with **30**. It's more than \"business as usual\" because we need to build new infrastructure, but it doesn't require scrapping our current AI models (overhaul).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* We need a standardized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Standardized High-Risk Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A unified software library for all R&D projects to implement the required automatic event recording and traceability logs.\n        *   How: Engineering will develop an internal API that captures timestamps, input data, and user IDs across all high-risk models. This will be integrated into our CI/CD pipeline as a mandatory requirement for deployment.\n        *   Why: It ensures consistent compliance across different research teams and prevents manual errors in record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation/Governance.* We need to handle the technical documentation side (Recital 71).\n        *   Title: Living Technical Documentation Registry.\n        *   What: A centralized, version-controlled repository for storing general characteristics, algorithms, and validation processes of every high-risk system.\n        *   How: We will implement a mandatory \"Compliance Ledger\" where researchers must document data sources and risk management updates in real-time. This registry will be synced with our model weights to ensure the documentation reflects the actual deployed version.\n        *   Why: It facilitates rapid responses to regulatory audits and satisfies Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Audit/Verification.* We need a way to check if the logs actually work (Article 12(2)).\n        *   Title: Post-Market Monitoring Simulation Suite.\n        *   What: A testing environment designed to simulate failures or substantial modifications to verify that logging capabilities capture these events accurately.\n        *   How: The QA team will create a \"stress-test\" protocol t