xperiments to ensure compliance without manual overhead.\n    *   *Proposal 2: Theoretical Research into Auditability.* Research \"interpretable logs\" or \"provenance tracking\" as a scientific contribution to the field of AI safety and transparency.\n    *   *Proposal 3: Governance/Policy Interface.* Create a technical documentation pipeline that automatically maps R&D outputs to EU AI Act requirements for seamless compliance reporting.\n\n    *   *Refining Proposal 1 (Infrastructure):*\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software layer integrated into our training and deployment pipelines to capture required event logs automatically.\n        *   How: We will develop a middleware API that intercepts system calls and data inputs to record timestamps and reference databases in a secure, immutable ledger.\n        *   Why: It minimizes researcher friction by automating the mundane aspects of regulatory record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Refining Proposal 2 (Research):*\n        *   Title: Research on Verifiable AI Provenance\n        *   What: A research project investigating cryptographic methods to ensure the integrity and traceability of high-risk AI system logs.\n        *   How: The team will explore hashing chains and zero-knowledge proofs to verify that recorded events have not been tampered with post-hoc.\n        *   Why: This elevates a regulatory requirement into a contribution to the scientific frontier of trustworthy AI.\n\n    *   *Refining Proposal 3 (Documentation/Mapping):*\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An automated system that transforms experimental metadata and version control history into formal technical documentation as defined by Recital 71.\n        *   How: We will build a tool that parses git commits, model cards, and dataset manifests to generate living compliance documents in real time.\n        *   Why: It ensures our intellectual merit i