., better interpretability and auditing tools).\n\n    *   Reasoning: The lab must transition from ad-hoc experimentation logs to systematic, automated traceability frameworks integrated directly into our model architectures. Since this regulation targets \"high-risk\" systems, we should treat record-keeping not as an administrative afterthought but as a technical requirement for deployment viability. By formalizing these logging standards, we can actually improve the intellectual merit of our research through enhanced reproducibility and forensic analysis of AI failures. This ensures our scientific contributions remain legally viable and industrially applicable within the European market.\n    *   Stance (Number): 25. (It's not a major overhaul of *what* we study, but it is a significant change in *how* we implement and document it).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: A technical framework for automated logging.*\n        *   Title: Automated Traceability Framework for High-Risk AI\n        *   What: An internal software library that standardizes event logging across all high-risk model prototypes. It ensures metadata like timestamps, input hashes, and versioning are captured automatically.\n        *   How: We will develop a middleware layer that intercepts API calls and weights updates to log them into an immutable ledger. This system will be integrated into our existing ML Ops pipeline.\n        *   Why: It guarantees compliance with Article 12 without requiring researchers to manually track every event. (Count check: ~75 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Research on \"Audit-Ready\" architectures.*\n        *   Title: Research on Intrinsic Model Auditability and Forensics\n        *   What: A research project focusing on creating AI architectures that naturally produce human-interpretable logs of their internal decision logic. This moves beyond simple event logging to semantic traceability.\n        *   How: The team will explore self-explaining neural networks and attention