 Score: 20-30. It's not \"business as usual\" because we must allocate resources to these features, but it's not a pivot in our scientific mission. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure/Framework.*\n        *   Title: Standardized Automated Logging Framework (SALF)\n        *   What: A universal software library integrated into all high-risk project templates to automate event recording.\n        *   How: Engineers will build a middleware layer that captures timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs by default. This ensures consistency across different R&D teams without manual implementation per project.\n        *   Why: It minimizes the risk of human error in compliance and streamlines regulatory audits.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Governance (Documentation).*\n        *   Title: High-Risk AI Traceability Audit Protocol\n        *   What: A mandatory internal review process to verify that technical documentation matches actual system logs.\n        *   How: Compliance officers will conduct quarterly \"stress tests\" on the logging capabilities of active projects. We will map specific R&D milestones to the requirements in Recital 71.\n        *   Why: This safeguards our market reputation by proving we can actually produce the required records upon request.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: New Research/Capability (Innovation).*\n        *   Title: Privacy-Preserving Audit Log Research\n        *   What: A research project to develop logs that satisfy Article 12 while protecting sensitive data via differential privacy or encryption.\n        *   How: The lab will investigate cryptographic hashing and secure enclaves for storing the required \"input data\" records. This transforms a regulatory burden into a competitive technical advantage.\n        *   Why: It allows us to be compliant without compromising our commitments to data privacy or institutional IP.\n\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Number 0-100? Yes (25).\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/Concrete? Yes (Framework, Audit Protocol, Privacy Research).\n    *   Title $\\le 10$ words? Yes.\n    *   What (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   How (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   Why (1 sentence)? Yes.\n    *   Length per proposal 60-110 words?\n        *   P1: ~75 words.\n        *   P2: ~70 words.\n        *   P3: ~80 words.\n    *   No bullet lists inside fields? Yes.\n    *   Role maintained? Yes.\n\n    *   Wait, the prompt says \"how much should the lab change its R&D agenda\". The *agenda* usually refers to what we are researching. Logging is a technical requirement for deployment. If our agenda was just \"pure theory,\" this forces it toward \"applied/deployable.\" But if we already do high-risk AI, it's an engineering shift. I'll stick with 25.\n\n    *   Let's refine the proposals to be very precise.\n    *   P1: Focus on the technical implementation of logging.\n    *   P2: Focus on the governance/verification side.\n    *   P3: Focus on solving a conflict (logging vs privacy) as an R&D project.",
