nce Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized software library that automatically generates EU AI Act compliant logs across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop a middleware API that captures timestamps, input data, and user IDs by default. This tool will be integrated into the mandatory CI/CD pipeline for all production deployments.\n        *   Why: It ensures uniform compliance and reduces the burden on individual researchers to implement logging manually.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Governance/Audit Process.* We need a way to verify that these logs exist and are accurate before release.\n        *   Title: Pre-Deployment Regulatory Audit Protocol.\n        *   What: A formal internal review process to certify that a system's record-keeping capabilities meet Article 12 standards.\n        *   How: Compliance officers will conduct \"log-stress tests\" where they simulate failure events and verify the resulting logs against regulatory requirements. No high-risk system can move to beta testing without a signed compliance certificate.\n        *   Why: This mitigates legal risk by preventing non-compliant systems from ever entering the market.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Research into \"Explainable Logging\" or Forensic AI.* Turn the constraint into an R&D opportunity (market reputation).\n        *   Title: Advanced Traceability and AI Forensics Project.\n        *   What: An R&D initiative to develop next-generation logging that provides deeper semantic insight into why a system took a specific action.\n        *   How: The lab will research methods for linking low-level event logs with high-level model interpretations using causal tracing. This moves the lab beyond basic compliance toward industry leadership in AI transparency.\n        *   Why: It transforms a regulatory burden into a competitive market advantage and enhances our reputation for safety.\n\n    *   *Reasoning check:* 3-5 sentences? Yes. (3 sentences).\n    *   *Sco