        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nTechnical viability of solutions, speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n        *   Recital 71: Emphasis on traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring, and automatic recording (logs). Technical documentation must be up-to-date throughout the lifetime.\n        *   Article 12: High-risk AI systems *must* allow for automatic event logging. Specifically focuses on risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. Annex III point 1(a) has specific requirements (timing, databases used, input data, human verifiers).\n\n        *   Compliance Officer (35): Compliance by design, integrate telemetry into pipelines.\n        *   Lab Director (40): Infrastructure problem, centralize tools to minimize researcher burden.\n        *   Researcher (45): AI observability/reproducibility opportunity.\n(1) Reason in 3-5 sentences on response; (2) Stance score (0-100); (3) Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer, I care about *how* this gets built without slowing down prototyping. If we just add \"compliance tasks\" to every sprint, speed drops.\n    *   The best approach is building reusable \"logging sidecars\" or middleware that handle Article 12 requirements automatically so the researchers don't have to think about it.\n    *   I agree with the Lab Director on centralization but focus more on the *technical implementation* (the \"how\") rather than just the administrative goal.\n    *   The stance score should reflect a need for technical adjustment, but not a complete change in the R&D agenda (we're still making AI, just adding logging). Probably around 30-50.\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* We need to implement these requirements as automated infrastructure components rather than manual checklists to avoid killing prototyping speed. By developing standardized, modular logging wrappers that can be plugged