n pipeline using tools like Sphinx or MkDocs linked to our CI/CD triggers.\n        *   Why: It ensures the \"up to date\" requirement of Recital 71 is met without slowing down iterative research cycles.\n\n    *   Reasoning (3-5 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   Number (0-100)? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/Concrete? Yes (Infrastructure, Research, Process).\n    *   Title (<10 words)? Yes.\n    *   What (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   How (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   Why (1 sentence)? Yes.\n    *   Length per proposal (60-110 words)?\n        *   P1: ~55 words (Too short, need to expand).\n        *   P2: ~60 words (Borderline).\n        *   P3: ~55 words (Too short).\n    *   No bullet lists inside fields? Yes.\n    *   Plain prose? Yes.\n\n    *   *P1 refined:*\n        Title: Unified Automated Traceability Infrastructure\n        What: A centralized, standardized logging library integrated into all laboratory model pipelines to automate event recording across different projects.\n        How: We will develop a software middleware layer that captures hyperparameters, data versions, and runtime events consistently across frameworks like PyTorch or JAX. This tool will automatically generate timestamps and reference IDs for every system execution.\n        Why: It minimizes manual overhead for researchers while guaranteeing regulatory compliance through systemic design rather than individual effort. (78 words)\n\n    *   *P2 refined:*\n        Title: Semantic Event Logging Research\n        What: A research project to develop AI-driven summaries of system logs that translate raw event data into human-comprehensible traceability reports.\n        How: We will utilize specialized large language models to analyze high-dimensional log data and map those patterns to the risk categories defined in Article 79(1). This involves creating a new taxonomy for AI failure modes based on log signatures.\n        Why: This advances the knowledge frontier regard