        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The lab must shift from a purely performance-driven R&D approach to one of 'compliance by design' to ensure institutional survival. We need to integrate automated logging and rigorous documentation into the actual architecture of our high-risk AI systems rather than treating them as afterthoughts. Failure to do so risks massive fines and severe damage to our market reputation during regulatory audits. Consequently, we must formalize our data lineage and event recording processes immediately.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Standardized Compliance Logging Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A centralized software library for automated event recording and timestamping designed specifically for our high-risk AI systems. This framework will provide a uniform interface for all R&D teams to implement required logging capabilities.\",\n      \"how\": \"Engineers will integrate this API into every model pipeline to capture input data, usage periods, and database references automatically. We will deploy it as a mandatory dependency in the internal development environment.\",\n      \"why\": \"This ensures uniform adherence to Article 12 across disparate R&D projects to avoid fragmented compliance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Registry\",\n      \"what\": \"An automated, version-controlled repository for tracking model characteristics, data lineages, and validation processes in real time. It replaces static reports with a live database of system specifications.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement a mandatory documentation template integrated with our Git workflow to update records whenever parameters or training sets change. Compliance officers will review these logs weekly.\",\n      \"why\": \"This facilitates the traceability required by Recital 71 and streamlines regulatory audits for institutional survival.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Post-Market Risk Detection Suite\",\n      \"what\": \"A specialized toolset for analyzing system logs to detect anomalies that signal substantial modifications or emergent safety risks. This serves as an early warning system for regulatory breaches.\",\n      \"how\": \"Data scientists will build anomaly detection monitors that alert the compliance office when system behavior deviates from the validated baseline. These tools will scan production logs continuously.\",\n      \"why\": \"This protects our market reputation by identifying failures before they result in severe legal sanctions.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nInstitutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\nEU AI Act, specifically Article 12 and Recital 71 regarding record-keeping/logging for high-risk AI systems.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how to respond.\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) indicating the degree of R&D agenda change.\n        *   (3) Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals.\n            *   Format for each: Title (max 10 words), What (1-2 sentences), How (1-2 sentences), Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words total across fields.\n            *   No bullet lists inside fields, plain prose only.\n\n    *   *Article 12:* Focuses on \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" for high-risk AI systems.\n    *   *Recital 71:* Emphasizes traceability, technical documentation (characteristics, algorithms, data, training/testing), and post-market monitoring.\n    *   *Specifics:* Need to log start/end times, reference databases, input matches, and human verifiers for certain categories.\n\n    *   The lab is an R&D institution. Compliance is non-negotiable for survival (avoiding fines) and reputation.\n    *   R&D often prioritizes performance over logging; this requires a shift toward \"compliance by design.\"\n    *   We must integrate automated logging into the architecture of any system that could be classified as high-risk.\n\n    *   Does it change *what* we build (the core science/goals)? Not necessarily. It changes *how* we build it and adds overhead.\n    *   However, if current projects are \"black boxes\" with no logging, significant engineering effort is needed.\n    *   Score: ~30-40. It's not a major overhaul of the *agenda* (we still build AI), but it's a mandatory modification of the *workflow*. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Engineering.* We need a standardized logging framework across all projects.\n        *   Title: Standardized Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A centralized software library for automated event recording and timestamping.\n        *   How: Engineers will integrate this API into every high-risk model pipeline to capture input data and usage periods automatically.\n        *   Why: This ensures uniform adherence to Article 12 across all disparate R&D projects.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation/Process.* We need a system for the technical documentation mentioned in Recital 71.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Registry\n        *   What: An automated version-controlled repository for tracking model characteristics, data lineages, and validation processes.\n        *   How: We will implement a mandatory template integrated with our Git workflow to update documentation whenever parameters change.\n        *   Why: It facilitates the traceability required by Recital 71 and streamlines regulatory audits.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Monitoring/Post-Market.* We need tools for \"post-market monitoring\" mentioned in Art 12(2)(b).\n        *   Title: Post-Market Risk Detection Suite\n        *   What: A specialized toolset to analyze logs for anomalies that signal substantial modifications or safety risks.\n        *   How: Data scientists will build anomaly detection monitors that alert compliance officers when system behavior deviates from the validated baseline.\n        *   Why: This protects our reputation by identifying failures before they lead to regulatory sanctions.\n\n    *   *Reasoning (3-5 sentences):* Check.\n    *   *Number (0-100):* Check (35).\n    *   *Exactly 3 proposals:* Check.\n    *   *Distinct/Concrete:* Yes (Infrastructure, Documentation, Monitoring).\n    *   *Titles (max 10 words):* Check.\n    *   *What (1-2 sentences), How (1-2 sentences), Why (1 sentence):* Check.\n    *   *Word count per proposal (60-110 words):* Let's double-check the counts.\n\n    *Proposal 1:*\n    Title: Standardized Compliance Logging Framework (4)\n    What: A centralized software library for automated event recording and timestamping across all high-risk systems. (15)\n    How: Engineers will integrate this API into every model pipeline to capture input data, usage periods, and database references automatically. (20)\n    Why: This ensures uniform adherence to Article 12 across disparate R&D projects. (11)\n    Total: ~50 words. *Too short.* Needs expansion.\n\n    *Revised Proposal 1:*\n    Title: Standardized Compliance Logging Framework\n    What: A centralized software library for automated event recording and timestamping designed specifically for our high-risk AI systems. This framework will provide a uniform interface for all R&D teams to implement required logging capabilities.\n    How: Engineers will integrate this API into every model pipeline to capture input data, usage periods, and database references automatically. We will deploy it as a mandatory dependency in the internal development environment.\n    Why: This ensures uniform adherence to Article 12 across disparate R&D projects to avoid fragmented compliance.\n    *Count check:* Title (4) + What (30) + How (29) + Why (16) = 79 words. *Perfect.*\n\n    *Proposal 2:*\n    Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Registry\n    What: An automated, version-controlled repository for tracking model characteristics, data lineages, and validation processes in real time. It replaces static PDF reports with a live database of system specifications.\n    How: We will implement a mandatory documentation template integrated with our Git workflow to update records whenever parameters or training sets change. Compliance officers will review these logs weekly.\n    Why: This facilitates the traceability required by Recital 71 and streamlines regulatory audits for institutional survival.\n    *Count check:* Title (4) + What (28) + How (30) + Why (16) = 78 words. *Perfect.*\n\n    *Proposal 3:*\n    Title: Post-Marke