ghtly higher than the others because I see a research angle, but still recognizing that the *core* goals of the lab shouldn't shift entirely to compliance engineering.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Research-driven Logging.* Instead of just \"logs for regulators,\" create \"logs for researchers.\"\n        *   Title: AI Observability Framework for Scientific Reproducibility.\n        *   What: A high-fidelity telemetry system that captures latent states and decision paths alongside the mandatory regulatory logs.\n        *   How: Integrate hooks into our model architectures to export internal activation patterns and attention maps during execution.\n        *   Why: This elevates compliance data into a rich dataset for studying emergent behaviors in high-risk systems.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Automation of Documentation.* The text mentions \"technical documentation... kept up to date.\" Let's automate this via AI (meta-AI).\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Generator.\n        *   What: A tool that parses training logs and code commits to auto-generate the technical documentation required by Recital 71.\n        *   How: Develop an LLM-based pipeline that maps version control history and experiment trackers to the specific EU AI Act reporting templates.\n        *   Why: It reduces the administrative burden on researchers, allowing more time for actual scientific discovery.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Verification/Auditability Research.* Article 12(3)(d) mentions natural persons verifying results. Let's research *how* that verification happens.\n        *   Title: Human-AI Interaction Study for Regulatory Verification.\n        *   What: An empirical study on how human operators interact with logs to verify AI outputs as required by the Act.\n        *   How: Design a series of user-centric experiments measuring the efficacy of different logging interfaces in detecting system failures.\n        *   Why: This transforms a legal requirement into a peer-reviewed contribution to the field of Human-Computer Interaction.\n\n    *   Role: Researcher? Yes.\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes (4).\n    *   Stance number 0-100? Yes (40).\n    *   Exactly 3 distinct proposals? Yes.\n    *   Fields: title, what, how, why? Yes.\n    *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words?\n        *   Prop 1: ~75 words.\n        *   Prop 2: ~75 words.\n        *   Prop 3: ~80 words.\n    *   Plain prose (no bullet lists inside)? Yes.\n    *   No internal bullets? Checked.",
