es into the R&D process requires effort. It's not a \"major overhaul\" of the scientific agenda, but it is more than \"business as usual.\"\n    *   Score: 20-30. Let's go with 25. It's an adjustment to *how* we work and some new side projects, but not a pivot in our core research goals.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical/Infrastructure.* A standardized logging framework for high-risk models.\n        *   Title: Unified Traceability Framework for High-Risk Architectures.\n        *   What: An internal API and database schema designed to automate the recording of events throughout a model's lifecycle.\n        *   How: We will implement middleware that intercepts input/output streams and metadata, storing them in an immutable ledger.\n        *   Why: This ensures consistent compliance across all lab projects without requiring researchers to reinvent logging for every experiment.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Theoretical/Research.* Researching \"Explainable Logging\" or \"Provable Traceability.\" Let's focus on the scientific impact of observability.\n        *   Title: Research into High-Fidelity Observability for AI Safety.\n        *   What: A research project exploring how granular event logs can be used to detect emergent risky behaviors in real-time.\n        *   How: We will develop new metrics and anomaly detection algorithms that operate specifically on the log data required by Article 12.\n        *   Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution toward AI safety and interpretability.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Process/Documentation.* Automating the technical documentation mentioned in Recital 71.\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: A tool that automatically generates the required technical documentation by pulling data from training logs and version control.\n        *   How: We will create a pipeline that parses hyperparameters, dataset manifests, and validation results into EU-compliant reports.\n       