epth is dynamically toggled based on the project maturity level, moving from exploration to high-risk deployment. This prevents overhead during early discovery.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement environment-based configurations that switch between minimal debug logs for initial R&D and full regulatory logging for production candidates. The transition triggers a mandatory audit check of the logging schema.\",\n      \"why\": \"This protects our research agility while ensuring a seamless, documented transition to full legal compliance.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nTechnical viability of solutions, speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n        *   Recital 71 & Article 12: Mandatory automatic recording of events (logging) for high-risk AI systems.\n        *   Purpose: Traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring.\n        *   Specific requirements for certain Annex III systems (time of use, reference DBs, input data matches, human verifier ID).\n\n        *   Compliance Officer (25): High priority on architectural integration to avoid fines/retroactive fixes.\n        *   Lab Director (35): Focus on standardized shared infrastructure to minimize resource waste and maintain momentum.\n        *   Researcher (40): Frame as an opportunity for observability/reproducibility science.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer, my primary concerns are *prototyping speed* and *technical viability*.\n    *   Regulations like this can slow down the \"fail fast\" cycle if every prototype needs full EU AI Act logging from day one.\n    *   However, I know that \"bolting on\" logging later is a technical nightmare (refactoring architecture).\n    *   My stance should be pragmatic: we need it, but it must be frictionless. If the tools are clunky, developers will bypass them or prototyping speed will tank.\n\n    *   Reasoning: Focus on the tension between rapid prototyping and rigorous logging. Advocate for \"logging-as-a-service\" or middleware so that researchers aren't writing boilerplate log code. Emphasize that technical viability depends on an automated pipeline rather than manual documentation.\n    *   Stance Score: Around 45-55. It's a moderate adjustment because it doesn't change *what* we research (the models), but it significantly changes *how* we build them (the plumbing).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: The \"Plumbing\" approach.* Create a standardized logging middleware/API. This aligns with the Lab Director's shared infrastructure idea but focuses on the *developer experience (DX)* and API design to maintain prototyping speed.\n    *   *Idea 2: Automated Documentation Pipeline.* Linking logs to technical documentation automatically. Recital 71 mentions \"keeping records... in a clear and comprehensive form.\" A tool that generates compliance reports from system logs reduces manual overhead for the engineers.\n    *   *Idea 3: The \"Shadow-Logging\" Sandbox.* A development environment where logging is optional/lightweight during initial exploration but automatically switches to high-fidelity mode when a project transitions to \"High-Risk candidate\" status. This preserves the \"fail fast\" speed of early R&D while ensuring a clear path to compliance for viable systems.\n\n    *   *Check constraints:* 3-5 sentences for reasoning. Stance score (0-100). Exactly 3 proposals. Each proposal: title (<10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence). Word count per proposal: 60-110 words. No bullet lists inside fields. Plain prose.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:* Title: Unified Logging API Middleware. What: A lightweight wrapper for all AI modules that standardizes event capture without requiring custom code for every new prototype. How: We build a shared library with pre-defined hooks for start/end times and input metadata that integrates directly into our existing ML frameworks. Why: It prevents compliance from slowing down the prototyping cycle by removing boilerplate logging tasks.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:* Title: Automated Compliance Report Generator. What: A tool that parses system logs to automatically populate the technical documentation required by Recital 71. How: We develop a pipeline that maps raw log events to regulatory requirements and exports them into standardized PDF or Markdown reports. Why: It eliminates the manual burden of record-keeping for engineers during the validation phase.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:* Title: Tiered Logging Lifecycle Framework. What: 