s as usual\" because high-risk classification requires specific architectural changes.\n    *   Score: ~30-40. It's an engineering requirement, not a shift in the scientific mission. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Standardization/Infrastructure.* (Focus on speed of prototyping). A \"Compliance Logging Wrapper.\"\n        *   What: A standardized software library for automatic event logging across all high-risk prototypes.\n        *   How: Create a middleware layer that captures timestamps, inputs, and system states by default using an API.\n        *   Why: This removes the manual burden from developers and ensures consistent data formats for auditing.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation Automation.* (Focus on Recital 71's requirement for updated technical documentation). \"Auto-Doc Pipeline.\"\n        *   What: An automated system that generates technical documentation from code comments, config files, and training logs.\n        *   How: Implement a CI/CD pipeline tool that extracts hyperparameters and dataset versions into a formatted report.\n        *   Why: It maintains the required up-to-date documentation without slowing down the R&D cycle.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Monitoring/Observability Tooling.* (Focus on Article 12(2) - monitoring for risks). \"Post-Market Risk Dashboard.\"\n        *   What: A real-time observability platform to monitor high-risk systems for drift or anomalous behavior.\n        *   How: Build a telemetry dashboard that aggregates logs and alerts engineers when performance metrics hit critical thresholds.\n        *   Why: This fulfills the regulatory need for post-market monitoring while improving system reliability.\n\n    *   Check constraints: No bullets inside fields? Yes. Plain prose? Yes. Word counts? Let's refine.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 Refinement:*\n        Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        What: A standardized software library providing automated event recording for all high-risk AI prototypes