rchers and maximizes resource efficiency across all high-risk projects. By automating the compliance burden, we protect our funding from penalties without sacrificing our ambitious research goals.\n\n    *   The Compliance Officer wanted change (30). I see this more as an operational adjustment than a fundamental shift in *what* we are researching. It's about the \"how,\" not the \"what.\"\n    *   Score: 25/100. (It requires work, but it doesn't change our scientific agenda—only the engineering standards).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: The Infrastructure Approach (Centralized Logging).*\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Middleware.\n        *   What: A standardized software library that researchers integrate into their AI pipelines to automate event logging.\n        *   How: Our systems team will build a modular API that captures timestamps, input data references, and user IDs automatically.\n        *   Why: It prevents redundant effort across teams and ensures consistent regulatory adherence without manual record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: The Process Approach (Audit/Documentation).*\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Generator.\n        *   What: A tool that scrapes metadata from training runs to populate the required technical documentation for Recital 71.\n        *   How: We will integrate documentation hooks into our existing experiment tracking software to auto-generate compliance reports.\n        *   Why: This reduces the time researchers spend on paperwork, allowing them to focus on scientific discovery.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: The Resource Allocation/Funding Approach (Budgeting).*\n        *   Title: Regulatory Compliance Infrastructure Fund.\n        *   What: A dedicated slice of the annual budget specifically for maintaining the logging and traceability infrastructure.\n        *   How: We will reallocate a small percentage of overhead funding to create a permanent support team for AI Act maintenance.\n        *   Why