        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact.\n\n        *   Recital 71: Focus on traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring. Need for technical documentation (characteristics, algorithms, data, training, testing) and automatic logging of events.\n        *   Article 12: High-risk AI systems must allow automatic recording of events (logs). Specific goals: identifying risks/substantial modifications, post-market monitoring, operational monitoring. Special requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (time stamps, reference databases, input data matches, personnel IDs).\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  A number (0-100) reflecting how much the R&D agenda should change.\n        3.  Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n            *   Title (max 10 words).\n            *   What (1-2 sentences).\n            *   How (1-2 sentences).\n            *   Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words total.\n            *   Format: Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n\n    *   As a researcher focused on the knowledge frontier and scientific impact, I view regulatory compliance as a necessary baseline but not the primary driver of intellectual merit. However, \"traceability\" and \"automatic recording\" are actually interesting research problems (interpretability, observability, provenance).\n    *   The lab shouldn't stop its core R&D, but it needs to integrate these requirements into the development pipeline to ensure that high-risk systems can actually be deployed in the EU.\n    *   Response: Integrate logging and documentation as first-class citizens in the research lifecycle rather than an afterthought.\n\n    *   The regulation asks for *capabilities* (logging) and *documentation*. It doesn't forbid specific types of research or change the goal of \"better AI.\" It just adds a layer of bookkeeping.\n    *   Therefore, it shouldn't cause a \"major overhaul\" (100), but it can't be \"business as usual\" (0) because current R&D often ignores strict provenance and event logging in early prototypes.\n    *   A score around 20-30 seems right: the *what* stays the same, but the *how* (the process) changes. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical/Engineering solution.* A standardized logging framework for all high-risk projects to automate Article 12 compliance.\n    *   *Idea 2: Theoretical/Research project.* Researching \"Provable Traceability\" or \"Self-Documenting AI.\" This turns the regulation into a scientific opportunity.\n    *   *Idea 3: Policy/Process implementation.* A formal internal audit and documentation pipeline to ensure Recital 71 is met without slowing down research velocity.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (The Framework):*\n        *   Title: Unified Automated Event Logging Architecture\n        *   What: An internal software library that automatically captures system states, timestamps, and input-output pairs for all high-risk models.\n        *   How: We will integrate this middleware into our training and inference pipelines to create immutable logs. This ensures consistency across different research teams.\n        *   Why: It removes the manual burden of compliance from researchers while guaranteeing regulatory readiness.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 (The Research):*\n        *   Title: Forensic Interpretability for Post-Market Monitoring\n        *   What: A research project focused on developing methods to reconstruct a model's decision path from sparse logs.\n        *   How: We will apply counterfactual analysis and attention mapping to existing log data to identify risk triggers. The team will publish these findings in top-tier AI venues.\n        *   Why: It advances the scientific frontier of interpretability while directly satisfying Article 12 requirements.\n\n    *   