cumentation tools, we can satisfy the legal mandates without stifling scientific agility or draining too many resources from core R&D. The objective is to treat compliance as a utility service provided by the lab's infrastructure team, minimizing the cognitive load on the researchers.\n    *   *Stance:* I wouldn't change *what* we are researching (the agenda), but I would change how we allocate budget for tools. Maybe around 20-30? The Compliance Officer said 30. I'll go with 25 because while it's necessary, it shouldn't derail our scientific goals; it's an infrastructure cost, not a shift in research direction.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Centralized Infrastructure/Tooling (Efficiency focus).*\n        *   Title: Unified Logging and Traceability Framework\n        *   What: A standardized, API-driven logging service that all high-risk projects use to record events automatically.\n        *   How: The infrastructure team will develop a shared library and database backend for automated timestamping and input recording.\n        *   Why: It prevents every research team from wasting time building their own compliance tools.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Training (Resource allocation focus).*\n        *   Title: Compliance Integration Training for Lead Researchers\n        *   What: A targeted workshop series to integrate Article 12 requirements into the project planning phase.\n        *   How: We will create a mandatory checklist and template for technical documentation that researchers fill out during development milestones.\n        *   Why: This ensures compliance is handled incrementally, avoiding costly retrofitting at the end of a project.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: R&D/Innovation (Scientific ambition focus - turning a constraint into research).*\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Generator Research\n        *   What: A small internal project to develop AI tools that automatically extract and format technical documentation from code and experiment 