oals* of the AI, but the *process* of how we build and document them changes significantly. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling.* We need a standardized logging framework that every high-risk project must use.\n        *   Title: Standardized Automated Logging Framework (SALF)\n        *   What: A unified software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects.\n        *   How: Engineers will integrate this API into the model's runtime to capture timestamps, inputs, and human overrides automatically.\n        *   Why: This ensures uniform compliance across diverse R&D teams and prevents manual record-keeping errors.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Process/Documentation.* We need a living documentation system (not just a PDF at the end).\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An integrated versioning system that links code changes to technical documentation in real-time.\n        *   How: By using tools like Sphinx or MkDocs linked to Git commits, we will automate the update of capabilities and limitations.\n        *   Why: It fulfills Recital 71's requirement to keep documentation up to date throughout the system lifetime.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Auditing/Governance.* We need a way to verify that this is actually happening before things go to market.\n        *   Title: Internal Compliance Audit Protocol\n        *   What: A mandatory pre-deployment review process focusing specifically on traceability and logging capabilities.\n        *   How: The compliance team will perform \"traceability drills\" where they attempt to reconstruct a system's decision path using only the logs.\n        *   Why: This mitigates legal risk by identifying gaps in record-keeping before regulators conduct an official audit.\n\n    *   Reasoning (3-5 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   Number (0-100)? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Title (<10 words)? Yes.\n    *   What (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n   