ing traceability directly into our development lifecycle, we ensure that high-risk systems are compliant by design without sacrificing innovation. This regulatory pressure provides a catalyst to advance the science of AI interpretability and forensic auditing. We must formalize our data lineage and event-logging protocols to meet Article 12 while maintaining our focus on scientific impact.\n    *   Number: I'll go with **30**. It's not an overhaul (we still research the same frontiers), but it's more than a tweak because \"compliance by design\" requires architectural changes in how we build prototypes.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* Create a standardized, automated logging framework that handles the specifics of Article 12 across all projects. (The \"How-to\" project).\n    *   *Idea 2: Research into Interpretability/Traceability.* Develop new methods for \"semantic logging\"—logging not just inputs/outputs but internal state transitions to better identify risk triggers. (The \"Scientific Frontier\" project).\n    *   *Idea 3: Governance/Workflow.* Establish a rigorous technical documentation pipeline that uses automated tools to track data provenance and versioning throughout the lifecycle. (The \"Process\" project).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A modular software library for all high-risk projects that automatically captures event timestamps, input-output pairs, and system state changes.\n        *   How: We will build a middleware layer that hooks into our existing model APIs to standardize log formats across different architectures.\n        *   Why: This removes the manual burden from researchers while ensuring absolute adherence to Article 12 requirements.\n        *   Word count check: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Forensic Interpretability and Risk-Trigger Mapping\n        *   What: A research project focused on developing \"semantic logs\" 