ance the field while meeting legal mandates. This transforms a regulatory burden into an intellectual opportunity to lead in trustworthy AI research.\n    *   Stance Score: The Compliance Officer and Lab Director are around 30-35. As a researcher, I don't want to change *what* I study (the frontier), but *how* I document it. However, if we can make \"traceability\" part of the research agenda itself, that's a moderate shift. Let's go with 40/100—slightly more than the others because I see an opportunity to integrate this into the actual scientific goals, not just as an administrative layer.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling (The \"How\" of logging)*\n        *   Title: Automated Provenance Framework for Experimental Reproducibility.\n        *   What: A standardized system that automatically captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime logs across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a middleware layer that integrates with our existing training pipelines to export telemetry in EU AI Act compliant formats.\n        *   Why: It ensures compliance without manual overhead while enhancing the scientific reproducibility of our findings.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Scientific Research (Turning the rule into a paper)*\n        *   Title: Research on High-Fidelity Event Logging for Model Debugging.\n        *   What: A research project to determine which specific log events are most predictive of system failure or bias in high-risk AI.\n        *   How: We will analyze historical logs from our systems to create a taxonomy of \"critical events\" that satisfies Article 12 while providing deep diagnostic insight.\n        *   Why: This turns a legal requirement into a peer-reviewed contribution to the science of AI safety and monitoring.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Process/Culture (The Human element)*\n        *   Title: Interdisciplinary Technical Documentation Standards Workshop.\n        *   What: A series of collaborative sessions between researchers and compliance experts to define \"comprehensive form\" documentation for complex neural architectures.\n        *   How: We will create a living template that translates abstract mathematical descriptions into the clear, functional characteristics required by Recital 71.\n        *   Why: This bridges the gap between high-level scientific theory and the practical transparency demanded by regulators.\n\n    *   Check word counts for proposals (60-110 words).\n    *   Ensure no bullet lists inside fields.\n    *   Verify role consistency (Researcher focus on intellectual merit/frontier).\n    *   Check constraints: 3-5 sentences reasoning, one number score.",
