iness as usual\"? No, because we now have specific technical mandates for logging and traceability.\n    *   It's a moderate adjustment to the *process* and *infrastructure*, but not the *agenda*.\n    *   Score: 20-30 range. Let's go with 25. It's more about *how* we build than *what* we build, but it takes resources away from pure research.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* An automated logging framework for all high-risk projects.\n    *   *Idea 2: Policy/Governance.* A standardized documentation template and auditing workflow to ensure Recital 71 compliance.\n    *   *Idea 3: Research Pivot (The \"Scientific Ambition\" part).* Developing a new AI technique for *efficient* or *compressed* logging to reduce the storage overhead of these regulations.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified Automated Logging Infrastructure\n        *   What: A standardized software layer integrated into all R&D pipelines to automatically capture system events and input-output pairs.\n        *   How: We will develop a modular API that developers plug into their models to stream telemetry data to a secure, immutable database.\n        *   Why: This reduces the manual burden on researchers while ensuring regulatory traceability across different projects.\n        *   Check length: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Compliance-Integrated R&D Lifecycle Workflow\n        *   What: A new internal governance protocol that mandates technical documentation checkpoints during the design, training, and validation phases.\n        *   How: We will implement a gated review process where project funding is tied to the completion of traceability records.\n        *   Why: It prevents costly retrospective documentation efforts by embedding record-keeping into the natural development cycle.\n        *   Check length: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: Efficient Traceability Research Initiative\n        *   What: A research