e, shared logging infrastructure rather than requiring every project team to build their own from scratch. By treating these requirements as a standardized internal utility, we preserve scientific agility while mitigating regulatory risk.\n    *   *Stance Score:* Around 20-40. It's not a \"major overhaul\" of *what* we work on (the science stays the same), but it requires a shift in *how* we allocate resources for infrastructure. Let's go with 35 to align closely but slightly differently from the Compliance Officer, reflecting the operational cost concern.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Centralized Infrastructure.*\n        *   Title: Unified Regulatory Logging Middleware.\n        *   What: A shared software layer that automatically handles event recording and metadata tagging for all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a standardized API that project teams integrate into their pipelines to pipe logs to a secure, centralized vault.\n        *   Why: This prevents duplication of effort across different research teams and optimizes storage costs.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Resource Reallocation/Funding.*\n        *   Title: Compliance-Driven Infrastructure Grant Program.\n        *   What: A dedicated internal fund to offset the compute and storage costs associated with mandated record-keeping.\n        *   How: We will carve out a portion of the annual infrastructure budget specifically for the increased data footprints required by Article 12.\n        *   Why: This ensures that scientific ambition is not throttled by the cost of regulatory overhead.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Process/Training.*\n        *   Title: Technical Documentation Automation Framework.\n        *   What: A set of templates and scripts that auto-generate the required technical documentation from code comments and version control.\n        *   How: We will implement a toolchain that extracts hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results directly from MLflow or similar tracking