tation overhead, but the core R&D agenda (e.g., \"building better LLMs\" or \"medical AI\") remains. Let's go with **30**.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Implementation/Infrastructure.* We need an automated logging framework across all high-risk projects.\n        *   Title: Unified Automated Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software module integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to capture events and telemetry automatically.\n        *   How: Developers will implement a common API that records timestamps, input data, and system states to a secure, immutable ledger.\n        *   Why: This ensures immediate technical compliance with Article 12(1) across all current projects.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Governance/Documentation Process.* We need a way to manage the \"technical documentation\" mentioned in Recital 71.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Compliance Documentation System\n        *   What: A living repository that maps R&D decisions, training data, and validation processes directly to regulatory requirements.\n        *   How: The lab will adopt version-controlled documentation tools that trigger updates whenever a model is modified or retrained.\n        *   Why: It protects our market reputation by demonstrating transparent and rigorous development cycles to auditors.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Human-in-the-loop/Verification Tracking.* Specifically for Article 12(3)(d) regarding natural persons verifying results.\n        *   Title: Verifier Identity and Audit Trail Protocol\n        *   What: A specialized verification interface that captures the digital identity of researchers approving AI outputs.\n        *   How: We will integrate biometric or secure credentialing into the human-verification step of our high-risk workflows.\n        *   Why: This satisfies the specific record-keeping mandates for Annex III systems to prevent regulatory sanctions.\n\n    *   Check word counts and constraints.\n    *   No bullet lists inside fields.\n    * 