mated Risk-Event Detection Engine\",\n      \"what\": \"An AI-driven monitoring tool designed to scan system logs and trigger alerts when patterns indicating a substantial modification or high risk emerge. It moves us from passive logging to active monitoring.\",\n      \"how\": \"The team will develop anomaly detection algorithms specifically tuned to identify the risk situations described in Article 12(2)(a). These triggers will notify the compliance office in real time.\",\n      \"why\": \"This transforms a passive compliance burden into an active safety feature that protects our market reputation.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems under the EU AI Act.\nInstitutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning on response (3-5 sentences).\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) indicating how much the R&D agenda should change.\n        *   (3) Exactly three distinct, concrete proposals.\n            *   Title: max 10 words.\n            *   What: 1-2 sentences.\n            *   How: 1-2 sentences.\n            *   Why: 1 sentence.\n            *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words.\n            *   Format: Plain prose (no bullets inside fields).\n\n    *   *Recital 71:* Traceability is key. Need technical documentation (characteristics, algorithms, data, training/testing) and automatic logging of events throughout the lifetime.\n    *   *Article 12(1):* High-risk AI must technically allow for automatic logs.\n    *   *Article 12(2):* Logs must enable traceability regarding risks (Art 79), post-market monitoring (Art 72), and operational monitoring (Art 26).\n    *   *Article 12(3):* Specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (time stamps, reference DBs, input data matches, human verification IDs).\n\n    *   The lab needs to integrate logging as a core technical requirement rather than an afterthought.\n    *   Failure = fines or market exclusion (threat to survival/reputation).\n    *   The R&D agenda shouldn't change *what* they are researching in terms of AI capabilities, but *how* those systems are architected and documented. It's a process shift rather than a goal shift.\n\n    *   (1) Reasoning: We must transition from treating logging as an optional diagnostic tool to implementing it as a mandatory architectural requirement for all high-risk projects. This ensures we avoid prohibitive fines and maintain our reputation for reliability in the European market. Our technical workflows must now integrate automated traceability and meticulous documentation of training datasets and validation processes by default. Failure to comply risks total exclusion from key markets, threatening institutional survival.\n    *   (2) Number: 30 (It's a significant shift in *how* we build, but doesn't necessarily change the *goals* of the AI research itself).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* Create a standardized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Unified Automated Traceability Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all R&D pipelines to automate event logging and timestamping.\n        *   How: Engineers will develop a set of mandatory API hooks that record system inputs, outputs, and human interventions automatically across all high-risk models.\n        *   Why: This ensures consistency across diverse projects and minimizes the risk of manual reporting errors during audits.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Governance.* Technical documentation protocol.\n        *   Title: Living Documentation Protocol for High-Risk Systems\n        *   What: A mandatory, version-controlled documentation system that tracks the evolution of algorithms and training data throughout a system's lifetime.\n        *   How: We will implement a synchronized repository where researchers must update technical specifications and validation results before any model deployment stage.\n        *   Why: It provides the comprehensive traceability required by Recital 71 to prove compliance during regulatory inspections.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specialized R&D for Annex III (if applicable) or Risk Detection.* Focus on \"identifying situations that result in risk.\"\n        *   Title: Automated Risk-Event Detection Engine\n        *   What: An AI-driven monitoring tool designed to scan system logs and trigger alerts when patterns indicating a substantial modification or high risk emerge.\n        *   How: The team will develop anomaly detection algorithms specifically tuned to identify the \"risk situations\" described in Article 12(2)(a).\n        *   Why: This transforms a passive compliance burden into an active safety feature that protects our market reputation.\n\n    *   Check constraints:\n        *   Reasoning length? (4 sentences) - OK.\n        *   Number included? Yes.\n        *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n        *   Title < 10 words? Yes.\n        *   What/How/Why lengths? Yes.\n        *   No bullets inside fields? Yes.\n        *   Word count per proposal (60-110)?\n            *   Prop 1: ~65 words. OK.\n            *   Prop 2: ~68 words. OK.\n            *   Prop 3: ~72 words. OK.",
