        "reasoning_content": "Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nInstitutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\nEU AI Act - Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        *   High-risk AI systems must technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs).\n        *   Logs must enable traceability, identification of risks/modifications, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring.\n        *   Specific requirements for Annex III, point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference databases, matching input data, human verifiers).\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  A number (0-100) reflecting the extent of R&D agenda change.\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n            *   Each proposal must have: Title (max 10 words), What (1-2 sentences), How (1-2 sentences), Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Total length per proposal: 60-110 words.\n            *   No bullet lists inside fields.\n            *   Plain prose.\n            *   Roleplay as Compliance Officer.\n\n    *   As a Compliance Officer, I view this not as an opportunity for \"innovation\" but as a mandatory constraint. Failure to comply results in fines (threatening survival) and bad press (threatening reputation).\n    *   The regulation focuses on *how* the system is built and monitored (logging/traceability), not necessarily *what* the AI does (the core R&D goal), unless the current architecture makes logging impossible.\n    *   Response: Integrate these requirements into the development lifecycle immediately to avoid retrospective retrofitting, which is costly and risky.\n\n    *   The regulation demands specific technical capabilities (logging) but doesn't ban specific types of research or force a change in the *goal* of the AI systems. It changes the *engineering process*.\n    *   Therefore, it's not a \"major overhaul\" (100) nor is it \"business as usual\" (0).\n    *   It's a moderate adjustment to the technical roadmap—essentially adding a \"compliance layer\" to all high-risk projects.\n    *   Score: ~30-40. Let's go with 35. It's a significant engineering effort but doesn't change the *agenda* (the things we are trying to solve).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling.* We need a standardized logging framework so every researcher doesn't reinvent the wheel and miss a requirement.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all R&D pipelines to automate event recording.\n        *   How: The engineering team will build an API that captures timestamps, input data, and user IDs by default across all high-risk models.\n        *   Why: This ensures systemic compliance and eliminates the risk of human error during manual record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Policy/Process.* We need a way to identify which systems are \"high-risk\" as defined by the Act, otherwise we don't know where to apply Article 12.\n        *   Title: High-Risk AI Classification Protocol.\n        *   What: A formal internal auditing process to categorize every project according to EU AI Act risk tiers.\n        *   How: I will establish a review board that evaluates project specs against Annex III criteria before development begins.\n        *   Why: It prevents the lab from accidentally deploying non-compliant systems and focuses resources on the necessary controls.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Documentation/Lifecycle Management.* Recital 71 mentions technical documentation throughout the lifetime. We need a system for this \"living\" document.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Repository.\n        *   What: A centralized, version-controlled system for maintaining real-time technical documentation and risk management records.\n        *   How: We will implement a tool that links model versioning direct