usiness as usual\" because we need to dedicate specific engineering hours, but it's not an overhaul of the research agenda.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: A standardized logging framework.* (Engineering efficiency).\n        *   Title: Unified Regulatory Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A reusable software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a set of standardized APIs that integrate with our existing training pipelines to capture timestamps, inputs, and user IDs automatically.\n        *   Why: This prevents each research team from wasting resources building bespoke compliance tools.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Documentation automation.* (Resource efficiency).\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: A system that auto-generates the required technical documentation and version logs from codebase metadata.\n        *   How: We will implement CI/CD triggers that extract model parameters and training data references into a living document whenever a model is updated.\n        *   Why: This minimizes the administrative burden on scientists, allowing them to focus on core research.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Compliance Audit Review.* (Risk management).\n        *   Title: High-Risk System Classification Audit.\n        *   What: A formal review of our current portfolio to identify which projects fall under high-risk categories.\n        *   How: We will convene a cross-functional panel of legal experts and lead scientists to map existing project goals against Annex III criteria.\n        *   Why: Precise classification ensures we only allocate expensive logging resources to systems that actually require them.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 check:*\n        *   Title: Unified Regulatory Logging Framework (4 words) - OK.\n        *   What: A reusable software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk projects. (1 sentence) - OK.\n        *   How: We will develop a set of standa