    {\n      \"title\": \"Telemetry Resilience Stress-Testing Suite\",\n      \"what\": \"A set of automated testing scripts designed to verify that logging systems remain functional under extreme load or system failure. It ensures no critical events are dropped during crashes.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will build a test harness that simulates edge cases and hardware failures to validate the persistence and integrity of the automatic logs. These tests will be integrated into our CI/CD pipeline for every high-risk deployment.\",\n      \"why\": \"This guarantees the technical viability of our record-keeping systems before they face regulatory scrutiny.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution.\nTechnical viability of solutions, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) regarding high-risk AI systems (automatic logging, traceability, specific data for certain categories).\n\n        *   Compliance Officer: 35/100 (Non-negotiable constraints, standardized architecture to avoid fines).\n        *   Lab Director: 30/100 (Modular infrastructure, optimal compliance, don't stifle velocity).\n        *   Researcher: 40/100 (Catalyst for research into transparency/auditability).\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  Stance score (0-100): How much to change the R&D agenda?\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals:\n            *   Title (< 10 words).\n            *   What (1-2 sentences).\n            *   How (1-2 sentences).\n            *   Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Constraints: Each proposal 60-110 words total. No bullet lists inside fields. Plain prose.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer, I care about *implementation*.\n    *   I don't want \"compliance\" to mean slowing down the prototype cycle with manual paperwork.\n    *   I want tools that make this automatic so it doesn't get in the way of coding and testing.\n    *   If we have to do it, let's build a shared internal library/SDK so every new project inherits these capabilities without rewriting them. This maintains speed (prototyping) and ensures viability (it actually works).\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* We should avoid treating this as a manual documentation task and instead implement it as an automated middleware layer integrated into our standard development stack. By building reusable logging primitives, we can ensure that high-risk systems are compliant by default without slowing down the iterative prototyping cycle. This shifts the burden from the individual researcher to the underlying infrastructure, preserving technical velocity while satisfying the legal requirements for traceability.\n    *   *Stance:* 25/100. (The *agenda* doesn't change—we still build AI—but the *tooling* changes. It's a tactical shift, not a strategic overhaul of what we are researching).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Unified Logging SDK.*\n        *   What: A standardized internal library that abstracts EU AI Act logging requirements into simple API calls for all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a Python package providing decorators and wrappers to automatically capture timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs across different framework backends.\n        *   Why: This prevents developers from reinventing the wheel and ensures consistent data formats for auditability.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Automated Provenance Pipeline.*\n        *   What: An integrated pipeline that automatically links training datasets, model versions, and validation logs into a single immutable record.\n        *   How: We will use MLflow or DVC combined with a secure ledger to trigger automatic snapshots of the reference databases whenever a high-risk model is deployed.\n        *   Why: This streamlines the generation of technical documentation required by Recital 71 without manual intervention.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Compliance Stress-Testing Suite.*\n        *   What: A set of automated \"red-teaming\" scripts designed to verify that logging systems are functioning under failure conditions or high load.\n        *   How: We will build a test harness that simulates system crashes and edge cases to ensure no critical events are lost during high-risk operation.\n        *   Why: This guarantees the technical viability of our record-keeping systems before they face regulatory scrutiny.\n\n    *   Role check: Dev Engineer? Yes. (Focus on SDKs, pipelines, stress-testing).\n    *   Reasoning length: 3 sentences. Perfect.\n    *   Stance: 25/100. Logical.\n    *   Proposals count: Exactly 3. Distinct? Yes (Library vs Pipeline vs Testing).\n    *   Pro