 significant addition to the \"requirements.\" Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Engineering.* We need a standardized logging framework that every project uses so we don't have to reinvent it for every model.\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation/Governance.* A living technical documentation system that updates automatically or semi-automatically to satisfy the \"keep up to date\" requirement in Recital 71.\n    *   *Proposal 3: Audit/Verification Tooling.* A tool to analyze logs for risk identification (Article 12(2)(a)) so we can prove we are monitoring for risks.\n\n    *   *Drafting Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized API and database architecture implemented across all high-risk projects to automate event recording.\n        *   How: Engineers will integrate this middleware into the AI pipeline to capture timestamps, inputs, and system states by default.\n        *   Why: It ensures baseline regulatory compliance across diverse projects without duplicating engineering effort. (Word count check: ~55 words - need slightly more). Let's expand.\n        *   Revised What: A standardized API and database architecture implemented across all high-risk projects to automate the recording of system events over their lifetime.\n        *   Revised How: Software engineers will integrate this middleware into every AI pipeline to capture timestamps, input data, and internal system states by default.\n        *   (Word count check: ~65 words). Good.\n\n    *   *Drafting Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An automated system that links model training logs and version control to a living compliance document.\n        *   How: We will deploy tools that extract metadata from our Git repositories and experiment trackers into a structured, human-readable format.\n        *   Why: This minimizes the manual burden of maintaining up-to-date 