ed lab infrastructure.\n\n    *   Stance Score: 30/100. Why? It's not a \"major overhaul\" of *what* we work on (the science remains the same), but it requires a significant shift in *how* we build it (infrastructure adjustment). It's slightly higher than the Compliance Officer because from an infra perspective, implementing this across all high-risk projects is a non-trivial resource allocation shift. Actually, maybe 30 is right—it's a moderate correction to the pipeline, not a change in research goals.\n\n    *   Proposal 1: A shared infrastructure project (The \"Middleware\" approach).\n        *   Title: Universal Logging Middleware Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software layer that automatically captures events and metadata across different AI projects.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop a set of APIs that researchers plug into their models to handle timestamping and data lineage without writing custom code.\n        *   Why: This minimizes the time scientists spend on manual record-keeping, preserving our research velocity.\n\n    *   Proposal 2: A process/training project (The \"Standardization\" approach).\n        *   Title: High-Risk System Classification Protocol\n        *   What: An internal auditing system to determine which projects actually fall under Article 12 requirements.\n        *   How: We will implement a mandatory triage phase during the project proposal stage using a rubric based on Annex III categories.\n        *   Why: This prevents us from wasting expensive compute and storage resources on logging for low-risk experimental systems.\n\n    *   Proposal 3: A resource/funding project (The \"Budgetary\" approach).\n        *   Title: Compliance-Infrastructure Funding Reallocation\n        *   What: Shifting a portion of the current hardware budget toward dedicated long-term archival storage and auditing tools.\n        *   How: I will reallocate funds from non-critical compute expansions to acquire immutable st