.\n    *   Stance Score: 15/100. (It's not about changing *what* we research—the frontiers of AI don't change because of a logging requirement—but rather improving the technical scaffolding).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Scientific Provenance Framework.* Focus on the data and training side (Recital 71). Instead of just \"compliance logs,\" build a system for \"Scientific Lineage.\"\n    *   *Idea 2: Automated Telemetry Layer.* Address Article 12's automatic logging. Create a standardized API/wrapper that handles event logging without the researcher having to write it manually every time.\n    *   *Idea 3: Regulatory-Aware Evaluation Benchmarks.* Since high-risk systems need monitoring for \"risks\" (Art 12(2)(a)), create a set of \"adversarial compliance tests\" as part of the research pipeline.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Scientific Provenance and Lineage Framework\n        *   What: A system that automatically captures every version of data, hyperparameters, and model weights used in high-risk experiments.\n        *   How: We will implement a centralized metadata repository linked to our Git workflows and data lakes to create an immutable audit trail.\n        *   Why: This ensures regulatory compliance while enhancing the scientific reproducibility and intellectual merit of our publications. (Word count check: ~65 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Telemetry Wrapper\n        *   What: A standardized software library that provides an abstraction layer for automatic event logging across different AI architectures.\n        *   How: Researchers will wrap their model inference calls in this API, which automatically handles timestamps, input hashing, and system state recording.\n        *   Why: It removes the manual burden of record-keeping from researchers while satisfying Article 12 requirements for automatic logging. (Word count check: ~70 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: Dynamic Risk-Detection Bench