h-risk AI, they can't just stop; they must adapt. However, if current research ignores traceability entirely, some shift is needed to integrate \"compliance by design.\"\n    *   Score: 20-30. It's a significant operational change but not a pivot in the core R&D mission (unless the lab's goal was specifically non-traceable AI). Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical infrastructure for logging.* An automated system to handle logs across all high-risk projects.\n    *   *Idea 2: Documentation framework/standardization.* Creating templates and a living document system for Recital 71 requirements.\n    *   *Idea 3: Compliance Audit Tool / Monitoring System.* A way to check if the logs are actually capturing what Article 12 requires (specifically for Annex III cases).\n\n    *Refining Idea 3:* Maybe something more distinct? How about a training program or an internal \"Compliance-by-Design\" certification for researchers? Or a specific R&D project to automate the generation of technical documentation from code/data pipelines. Let's go with the Automated Documentation Pipeline.\n\n    *Proposal 1: Unified Logging Architecture.*\n    What: A standardized, immutable logging framework integrated into all high-risk AI development pipelines.\n    How: We will implement a centralized API that automatically captures event timestamps and system states across various R&D environments. This ensures consistency regardless of the specific model architecture used.\n    Why: It guarantees technical compliance with Article 12 while protecting us from claims of data tampering.\n\n    *Proposal 2: Dynamic Technical Documentation Repository.*\n    What: A living knowledge base that maps algorithms and training data to their respective risk-management profiles.\n    How: We will deploy a version-controlled documentation system where researchers link every model iteration to its training logs and validation results. This creates the traceability required by Recital 71.\n    Why: It 