ure without killing the research velocity.\n    *   I don't want a total overhaul of *what* we work on (the R&D agenda), but I need to adjust *how* we work on it (infrastructure).\n\n    *   (1) Reasoning: The lab needs to avoid \"compliance theater\" that drains resources from core research. We should focus on automating the logging and documentation requirements into our common infrastructure rather than making them manual burdens for each researcher. This ensures regulatory alignment while minimizing the impact on scientific output and funding efficiency.\n    *   (2) Stance Score: 30/100. (The *agenda*—the high-level research goals—doesn't change, but the operational overhead increases. It's more of a process shift than an agenda shift.)\n\n    *   Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling. A centralized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Logging Middleware\n        *   What: A standardized software layer integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event recording.\n        *   How: Develop a shared API that researchers plug into their training and inference loops to capture required metadata automatically.\n        *   Why: It reduces the per-project overhead for individual scientists, maintaining research velocity.\n\n    *   Proposal 2: Process/Documentation. Documentation templates/automation.\n        *   Title: Living Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An automated system that generates technical documentation from code commits and dataset versioning tools.\n        *   How: Integrate documentation generators with our Git repositories and data catalogs to auto-populate compliance reports.\n        *   Why: This prevents a massive, unfunded \"documentation sprint\" at the end of every project cycle.\n\n    *   Proposal 3: Strategic/Financial. Dedicated funding for \"Compliance R&D\".\n        *   Title: Regulatory Engineering Resource Allocation\n        *   What: A dedicated budget line and small team focused s