n efficiency than pure risk avoidance, but the requirement is mandatory. A \"moderate adjustment\" fits here because we aren't changing *what* we research (the AI goals), but *how* we build it (the infrastructure). Stance: 35/100.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Shared Infrastructure.* Create a standardized logging library so researchers don't have to build it manually every time.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Telemetry Layer\n        *   What: A shared software library that provides standardized, automatic event logging and audit trails across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will allocate two engineers to develop a plug-and-play API that handles timestamping, database referencing, and user identification automatically.\n        *   Why: This prevents every research team from wasting their own budget on redundant compliance engineering.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation Automation.* Instead of manual reports (which take forever), automate the technical documentation generation from code/config.\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: A system that extracts metadata, algorithm versions, and data lineages directly from our experiment trackers into regulatory-ready documents.\n        *   How: We will integrate our existing MLflow or Weights & Biases pipelines with a template generator for EU AI Act reports.\n        *   Why: It reduces the manual administrative burden on scientists, preserving their time for actual research.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Audit/Review Process (Governance).* A lean review gate to decide if a project is \"high-risk\" before spending too much effort on these requirements.\n        *   Title: High-Risk Classification Triage Framework\n        *   What: A streamlined internal protocol to determine early in the R&D cycle whether a system falls under Article 12's high-risk mandate.\n        *   How: We will establish a monthly cross-functional review board consisting of one lead researcher