k projects.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework (UCLF)\n        *   What: A centralized internal API and storage architecture that ensures all high-risk AI systems automatically capture required event logs.\n        *   How: Engineers will develop a shared library that wraps model execution in telemetry layers to record timestamps, inputs, and user IDs consistently.\n        *   Why: This prevents fragmented implementations across different teams and guarantees institutional auditability.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Policy/Process.* A mandatory \"Compliance Review Gate\" for R&D milestones.\n        *   Title: Regulatory Traceability Audit Protocol\n        *   What: An institutional mandate requiring a compliance sign-off on the record-keeping capabilities before any system moves from alpha to beta testing.\n        *   How: We will implement a checklist based on Article 12 and Recital 71 that developers must complete, verified by my office through technical demonstrations.\n        *   Why: It ensures no high-risk system reaches the market without meeting legal logging requirements.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Research/Innovation.* Developing \"Privacy-Preserving Traceability.\" (Since logs might contain sensitive data).\n        *   Title: Privacy-Enhanced Audit Log Research Project\n        *   What: An R&D initiative to create secure, encrypted logging mechanisms that satisfy EU traceability while protecting proprietary data and user privacy.\n        *   How: The lab will explore differential privacy or cryptographic hashing for the \"input data\" logs required by Article 12(3).\n        *   Why: This preserves our market reputation as a leader in both compliance and state-of-the-art privacy engineering.\n\n    *   Check constraints:\n        *   Reasoning (3-5 sentences)? Yes.\n        *   Number 0-100? Yes (35).\n        *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n        *   Distinct/concrete? Yes (Tech infrastructure, Process/Policy, New Research area).\n        *