e real-time reports on system capabilities and limitations.\n        *   Why: It reduces the administrative burden on researchers while guaranteeing the documentation remains up to date.\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   *Score:* Number 0-100? Yes.\n    *   *3 Proposals:* Yes.\n    *   *Distinct/Concrete:* Yes (Engineering vs. Scientific Research vs. Process Automation).\n    *   *Title length:* $\\le$ 10 words? Yes.\n    *   *What (1-2 sentences)?* Yes.\n    *   *How (1-2 sentences)?* Yes.\n    *   *Why (1 sentence)?* Yes.\n    *   *Length per proposal (60-110 words)?* Let's check.\n        *   P1: ~55 words. *Need to expand slightly.*\n        *   P2: ~60 words. *Borderline.*\n        *   P3: ~58 words. *Need to expand slightly.*\n    *   *No bullets inside fields?* Yes.\n    *   *Plain prose?* Yes.\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* The lab must integrate traceability and automatic logging into the foundational design of all high-risk AI projects rather than treating them as post-hoc additions. This transition requires a shift toward \"compliance-by-design,\" ensuring that every experimental iteration produces an immutable audit trail. By framing these requirements as scientific challenges in transparency, we can maintain our research momentum while meeting legal obligations.\n\n    *   *Score:* 25\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (Expanded):*\n        title: Unified High-Risk Logging Architecture\n        what: This is a standardized software library designed for automatic event logging across all high-risk AI projects in the lab. It provides a consistent interface to record system events throughout the model lifetime.\n        how: We will develop modular middleware that captures precise timestamps, input hashes, and system states into an immutable, queryable ledger. The team will integrate this library into our existing CI/CD pipeline for all production-track models.\n        why: This ensures consistent regulatory compliance across diverse research teams withou