? No. We aren't stopping AI research to do something else.\n    *   Is it business as usual? No, because we need new infrastructure for automatic logging and traceability.\n    *   It's more of an operational shift than a change in \"what\" the lab works on (scientific ambition). However, resource allocation will shift toward \"compliance engineering.\"\n    *   Score: 20-30. Let's go with 25. It's a significant implementation effort but doesn't change the scientific mission.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* A centralized logging framework. (Focus on efficiency).\n    *   *Idea 2: Process/Governance.* A classification and documentation audit system. (Focus on risk management).\n    *   *Idea 3: Scientific Research into \"Traceable AI\".* Turning a regulatory burden into a research opportunity (Explainability/Provenance). This balances scientific ambition with the constraint.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (The Tool):*\n        *   Title: Unified Telemetry and Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all high-risk model pipelines to automate event recording. It will ensure consistency across diverse projects by providing pre-built logging modules for inputs, timestamps, and system modifications.\n        *   How: The infrastructure team will develop a set of APIs that researchers wrap around their training and inference loops. This eliminates the need for individual teams to build custom compliance logs from scratch.\n        *   Why: It reduces redundant engineering effort across projects and ensures uniform regulatory compliance.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 (The Audit):*\n        *   Title: High-Risk AI Classification and Inventory System\n        *   What: A systematic internal review process to categorize every active project based on EU AI Act risk tiers. This system will map specific logging requirements to the corresponding high-risk categories, particularly for Annex III systems.\n        *   How: We will establish a