e. This approach minimizes rework and maintains our prototyping velocity.\n\n    *   The Compliance Officer wants 30, Lab Director 25, Researcher 20.\n    *   I don't want to overhaul the *agenda* (what we research), but I want to change the *tooling* (how we build). Overhauling the agenda means stopping AI research to do compliance research. That's a no. But adjusting the pipeline is necessary.\n    *   Stance: 15/100. (Minor adjustment to tooling/pipeline, not the core R&D goals).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: The \"Logging Sidecar\" / Centralized Infrastructure.*\n        *   Title: Unified Logging Middleware for High-Risk AI\n        *   What: A standardized API and sidecar container that automatically captures event logs without requiring researchers to write custom logging code.\n        *   How: We develop a lightweight wrapper around our model serving layer that intercepts inputs, outputs, and timestamps into a secure database.\n        *   Why: It decouples regulatory requirements from core research logic, preserving prototyping speed.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Automated Technical Documentation (The \"Doc-Gen\" Pipeline).*\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Documentation Generator\n        *   What: A tool that scrapes hyperparameters, dataset versions, and test results to automatically populate the technical documentation required by Recital 71.\n        *   How: We integrate metadata tracking into our experiment management system to export formatted reports at the push of a button.\n        *   Why: It eliminates manual record-keeping burdens that typically slow down the transition from prototype to product.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: A \"Risk-Classification\" Triage System (The Gatekeeper).*\n        *   Title: Risk-Aware Project Lifecycle Trigger\n        *   What: A technical triage system that classifies projects by risk level and automatically enables specific logging modules based on the classification.\n        *   How: We implement a project onboarding