doesn't need a major overhaul (it shouldn't stop being about the frontier), but the *methodology* and perhaps some specific *research directions* should shift.\n\n    *   Reasoning: I believe we should view these record-keeping requirements not merely as bureaucratic hurdles, but as an opportunity to advance the science of AI reproducibility and transparency. By integrating formal logging into our experimental pipeline, we can turn regulatory compliance into a tool for more rigorous scientific validation. We should avoid treating this purely as an engineering task and instead explore how automated traceability can reveal new insights into model behavior.\n\n    *   Stance Score: 15/100. (Why? Because the *agenda*—the high-level goals of pushing the frontier—remains the same, but we adjust the *approach*. It's a slight shift in how we do research, not what we are researching fundamentally.)\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* Focus on the \"automatic recording\" part. Create a standardized logging framework for all high-risk projects.\n    *   *Proposal 2: Scientific Research into Traceability.* Turn Article 12 into a paper. Research efficient, privacy-preserving logging for large-scale AI.\n    *   *Proposal 3: Data/Dataset Governance.* Focus on the \"reference database\" and \"input data match\" requirement (Article 12(3)). Build a versioned data lineage system.\n\n    *Refining Proposal 1 (The Tool):*\n    *   Title: Unified Traceability Framework for High-Risk AI.\n    *   What: A standardized internal library that automatically captures telemetry, input/output pairs, and timestamps across all R&D projects.\n    *   How: We will develop a middleware wrapper for our model training and inference pipelines to log events in a structured format without requiring manual researcher input.\n    *   Why: This removes the administrative burden while ensuring every experiment is inherently compliant and reproducible.\n\n    *Refining Proposal 2 (The Science):*\