        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"We must shift from an experimental-first mindset to one that integrates regulatory traceability directly into the development lifecycle. While this does not change our core scientific objectives, it requires a mandatory reallocation of engineering resources to build automated logging and documentation frameworks. Failure to comply risks significant legal penalties and would prevent the actual deployment of our high-risk systems in the European market.\",\n  \"stance\": 25,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A standardized and modular logging API integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to ensure consistent automatic event recording. This tool will act as the single source of truth for system behavior throughout the lifetime of any developed model.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will develop a middleware layer that captures timestamps, input references, and system states automatically across our diverse hardware clusters. The framework will be deployed as a mandatory library within our internal development environment.\",\n      \"why\": \"It prevents individual researchers from wasting expensive engineering hours building bespoke, non-compliant logging systems for every project.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Automated Technical Documentation Generator\",\n      \"what\": \"A tool that extracts training parameters and validation results to keep technical documentation current in real-time. This ensures our records evolve alongside the model iterations without manual intervention.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement a version-controlled metadata store linked directly to our existing experiment tracking systems like MLflow. The system will automatically generate reports based on predefined regulatory templates whenever a model is promoted to production.\",\n      \"why\": \"It reduces the manual administrative burden on scientists while strictly meeting