nge. Let's go with 25. It's more than \"business as usual\" because we need new tools, but far from a pivot.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Tooling/Infrastructure.* Create a standardized logging framework for all high-risk projects to ensure compliance by default.\n    *   *Idea 2: Research into the Logging itself (Intellectual Merit).* Explore \"efficient\" or \"verifiable\" logs that don't bloat the system but provide mathematical guarantees of traceability.\n    *   *Idea 3: Governance/Process.* A new internal audit protocol to map research iterations to technical documentation requirements.\n\n    *Refining Idea 2 for more \"Scientific Impact\":* Instead of just a tool, let's look at *automated provenance tracking* for training data and model versions, which aligns with the \"technical documentation\" part of Recital 71.\n\n    *Refining Idea 3:* Let's focus on the *privacy-utility trade-off* of logging natural persons (Article 12(3)(d)). How to log identification without violating GDPR or compromising security? This is a high-merit research problem.\n\n    *Proposal 1: Standardized Traceability Middleware.*\n    What: A modular software layer that automatically captures event logs and system metadata across different AI architectures.\n    How: We will develop an API-driven logging wrapper that integrates with existing training pipelines to record timestamps, input hashes, and database versions.\n    Why: This ensures baseline compliance without forcing every researcher to manually implement recording logic. (Word count check: ~65 words).\n\n    *Proposal 2: Formalized Provenance Mapping for Model Iterations.*\n    What: A research project focusing on the automated generation of technical documentation through version-controlled metadata.\n    How: The team will implement a system that links specific hyperparameters and dataset snapshots to final model performance metrics in a machine-readable graph.\n    Why: This transforms administrative record-keeping into a scientifi