ey aren't asking for a total overhaul of *what* is built, but how it's built).\n    *   From my perspective: I don't want to change *what* we research, but the *infrastructure supporting* that research needs an upgrade. It's not a \"major overhaul\" (100) or \"business as usual\" (0), but a necessary shift in tooling.\n    *   Score: 40/100. (Moderate adjustment to processes and infrastructure, though not the core scientific goals).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Unified Logging Framework.* Instead of every project doing its own logs, build one tool everyone uses.\n        *   Title: Standardized Traceability Middleware.\n        *   What: A modular software layer that automatically handles event logging and metadata capture across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a set of APIs that researchers plug into their models to standardize how timestamps, inputs, and user IDs are recorded.\n        *   Why: It reduces the individual engineering burden on research teams while ensuring uniform compliance.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Automated Documentation Pipeline.* Recital 71 mentions \"technical documentation\" kept up to date. Manual writing is a waste of time.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Generator.\n        *   What: A tool that auto-generates required technical documentation by pulling data directly from version control and experiment tracking logs.\n        *   How: We will integrate our existing CI/CD pipelines with doc-generation templates that map R&D artifacts to EU AI Act requirements.\n        *   Why: This minimizes the time researchers spend on administrative paperwork, maximizing their focus on scientific discovery.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Audit Sandbox/Validation Environment.* Need a way to verify these logs actually work before deployment without risking production systems.\n        *   Title: Compliance Validation Testbed.\n        *   What: A dedicated staging environment used specifically to stress-test the traceability and logging capabilities of high-risk AI systems.\n        *   How: We will allocate specific compute clusters where systems are subjected to simulated failures to ensure logs accurately capture the events for Article 12.\n        *   Why: It prevents costly late-stage redesigns by identifying logging gaps early in the development lifecycle.\n\n    *   Check word counts for proposals (60-110 words).\n    *   Check prose format (no bullets inside fields).\n    *   Ensure distinctness.\n    *   Confirm persona (Lab Director focused on efficiency/resources).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        Title: Standardized Traceability Middleware\n        What: A modular software layer that automatically handles event logging and metadata capture across all high-risk projects.\n        How: We will develop a set of APIs that researchers plug into their models to standardize how timestamps, inputs, and user IDs are recorded.\n        Why: It reduces the individual engineering burden on research teams while ensuring uniform compliance.\n        (Word count check: ~60 words. A bit tight. Let's expand slightly.)\n        *Revised Proposal 1:*\n        Title: Standardized Traceability Middleware\n        What: A modular software layer that automatically handles event logging and metadata capture across all high-risk projects.\n        How: We will develop a set of unified APIs that researchers plug into their model pipelines to standardize how timestamps, inputs, and user IDs are recorded.\n        Why: This approach reduces the individual engineering burden on research teams while ensuring uniform regulatory compliance without duplicating effort. (68 words)\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Generator\n        What: A tool that auto-generates required technical documentation by pulling data directly from version control and experiment tracking logs.\n        How: We will integrate our existing CI/CD pipelines with automated templates that map R&D artifacts