d, failing to comply risks legal penalties and prohibits the deployment of high-risk systems. Our response should prioritize scalable, platform-wide solutions over project-specific patches to maintain resource efficiency across all teams.\n    *   (2) Stance Number: 30 (It's a technical requirement/implementation detail rather than a change in *what* we research—the scientific ambition remains, but the *how* of implementation changes).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* Create a centralized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all model training and deployment pipelines to automatically capture event logs.\n        *   How: We will develop an API that researchers plug into their workflows to record timestamps, dataset versions, and input-output pairs without manual entry.\n        *   Why: It reduces the administrative burden on scientists while ensuring uniform compliance across diverse projects.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Documentation.* Automated technical documentation generation.\n        *   Title: Auto-Doc Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: A system that pulls metadata from experiment trackers and code repositories to generate live, updated technical documentation.\n        *   How: The team will implement scripts that convert MLflow or Weights & Biases logs into the comprehensive reports required by Recital 71.\n        *   Why: This preserves scientific ambition by eliminating time-consuming manual report writing.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specialized Feature for Annex III (High Risk).* A specific verification module for high-risk systems.\n        *   Title: Human-in-the-Loop Verification Audit Module\n        *   What: A specialized interface designed specifically to track and log the identities of natural persons verifying AI results.\n        *   How: We will build a secure authentication layer into our evaluation t