rely R&D and didn't think about logging, adding it to every project is a moderate shift in *workflow*, but maybe not the *agenda* (the goals). However, \"institutional survival\" means this becomes a primary goal. Let's say 25-30. It's an engineering requirement change.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Standardized Logging Framework.* Focus on the technical infrastructure for all high-risk projects.\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk AI Logging Architecture\n        *   What: A standardized software module integrated into all R&D pipelines to automate event recording and traceability.\n        *   How: We will develop a common API that captures timestamps, input metadata, and system states across different model architectures.\n        *   Why: This ensures baseline compliance for every project without reinventing the wheel each time.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Audit/Documentation Tooling.* Focus on Recital 71 (technical documentation).\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Documentation Engine\n        *   What: A tool that synchronizes real-time development logs with the required technical documentation for regulatory submission.\n        *   How: The system will automatically pull data from Git commits and training logs to populate a living compliance dossier.\n        *   Why: It reduces manual overhead and prevents reputation loss due to incomplete or outdated filings.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specific Implementation for Annex III (Biometrics/High Risk).* Focus on Article 12(3).\n        *   Title: Biometric Traceability Protocol for Annex III Systems\n        *   What: A specialized logging layer specifically designed for identity-matching systems to track database references and human verification.\n        *   How: We will implement a secure ledger that maps every match event to the specific reference dataset version and the operator ID.\n        *   Why: This addresses the strictest requirements of Article 12(3) to avoid catastrophic legal penalties.\n\n    *   *R