        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. Focus: technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        1.  Reasoning on lab response (3-5 sentences).\n        2.  Stance number (0-100) regarding change to R&D agenda.\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions), each with:\n            *   Title (max 10 words).\n            *   What (1-2 sentences).\n            *   How (1-2 sentences).\n            *   Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Word count per proposal: 60-110 words.\n            *   Format: Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n\n    *   Focus is on \"High-risk AI systems.\"\n    *   Requirement: Automatic recording of events (logs) throughout the lifetime.\n    *   Purpose: Traceability, risk identification, post-market monitoring, and specific requirements for certain Annex III types (timing, DB references, input data, human verification).\n    *   Technical documentation must be up to date.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer focused on prototyping speed, I see this as a potential bottleneck. Manual logging is slow; we need automated infrastructure.\n    *   We can't just \"add logs later\" if the architecture doesn't support it from the start.\n    *   The response should be to build standardized logging frameworks that wrap our prototypes so researchers don't have to reinvent the wheel for every experiment, maintaining speed while ensuring compliance.\n\n    *   Is this a major overhaul of *what* we work on (the AI models)? No.\n    *   Is it an overhaul of *how* we build them? Yes, moderately.\n    *   Score: 30-40. It's more about the \"plumbing\" than the core research goals. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Framework.* A standardized logging library for all prototypes. (Technical viability/speed).\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation Automation.* Auto-generating technical documentation from code and training logs. (Recital 71 requirement).\n    *   *Proposal 3: Compliance Auditing Tool.* A tool to verify if a prototype's logs meet Art 12 requirements before deployment. (Quality/Verification).\n\n    *Refining Proposal 1:*\n    - Title: Unified High-Risk Logging Middleware\n    - What: A standardized software wrapper that automatically captures timestamps, inputs, and system events across all R&D prototypes.\n    - How: We will develop a shared API layer that integrates with our existing model training pipelines to log telemetry without manual instrumentation by researchers.\n    - Why: This preserves prototyping speed by removing the burden of individual compliance implementation from the research teams.\n\n    *Refining Proposal 2:*\n    - Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n    - What: A system that syncs version control metadata and training logs into a live, human-readable technical dossier.\n    - How: We will use scripts to pull hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and validation results directly from our experiment trackers into structured reports.\n    - Why: It ensures the documentation remains up to date throughout the lifetime of the system as required by Recital 71.\n\n    *Refining Proposal 3:*\n    - Title: Compliance Verification Sandbox\n    - What: A testing environment designed to stress-test logging capabilities against specific EU AI Act Article 12 checklists.\n    - How: We will implement a suite of automated tests that simulate system failures or modifications to verify if the logs capture these events correctly.\n    - Why: This guarantees technical viability and compliance before a high-risk system moves toward post-market monitoring phases.\n\n    *   (1) Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   (2) Number 0-100? Yes (35).\n    *   3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Titles < 10 words? Yes.\n    *   What/How/Why constraints met? Yes.\n    *   Word count per proposal 60-110?\n        *   P1: ~70 words