e these record-keeping capabilities now will render our future systems unmarketable in the EU.\n    *   Score: 20-30. Why? It doesn't change *what* we are researching (e.g., if we research LLMs or Medical AI), but it changes *how* we build them. It's a structural adjustment, not a pivot of the R&D agenda itself. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Implementation/Infrastructure.* (The \"Logging Engine\").\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event logging.\n        *   How: Engineers will develop a middleware layer that captures timestamps, input hashes, and system state changes without manual intervention.\n        *   Why: This ensures baseline compliance across diverse projects while minimizing redundant engineering effort per model.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Documentation.* (The \"Compliance Audit Trail\").\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: An automated system that syncs R&D version control with the required technical documentation for Recital 71.\n        *   How: We will link Git commits and experiment trackers to a living compliance document that updates as algorithms change.\n        *   Why: It guarantees our documentation is kept up-to-date throughout the system lifetime, preventing audit failures.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specialized Compliance for Annex III (High Risk).* (The \"Specifics\").\n        *   Title: Biometric Traceability Audit Module.\n        *   What: A specialized verification tool specifically for systems under Annex III 1(a) to track human-in-the-loop approvals.\n        *   How: We will implement a cryptographically signed identity log that records which natural person verified specific system matches.\n        *   Why: This satisfies the most stringent requirements of Article 12(3) and protects the lab from liability regarding unauthorized usage