roposal 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling.* Create an open-source logging framework that handles the EU requirements but is designed for researchers.\n        *   Title: Open-Source AI Observability Framework\n        *   What: A standardized, modular logging library specifically tailored to meet Article 12's traceability requirements while maintaining research flexibility.\n        *   How: We develop a middleware layer that captures system events and input/output matches across different model architectures into a queryable database.\n        *   Why: This transforms a compliance burden into a reusable tool for the broader scientific community to improve transparency.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Research Project on Interpretability.* Use the required logs to study high-risk system behavior in real-time.\n        *   Title: Longitudinal Behavioral Analysis of High-Risk AI\n        *   What: A research program utilizing mandatory Article 12 event logs to identify emergent failure modes and drift in deployed systems.\n        *   How: We will apply statistical anomaly detection to the automated logs to map the gap between expected performance and real-world outcomes.\n        *   Why: It leverages regulatory data to produce high-impact publications on AI reliability and safety.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Policy/Standardization Work.* Lead the definition of what \"appropriate traceability\" means for specific scientific domains.\n        *   Title: Standardizing Traceability Metrics for Scientific AI\n        *   What: A collaborative initiative to define the minimum viable logging parameters that satisfy regulators without compromising research agility.\n        *   How: We will publish a series of white papers and engage with EU regulatory bodies to codify technical standards for \"meaningful\" event logs.\n        *   Why: Establishing these standards ensures our lab shapes the rules rather than merely reacting to them.\n\n    *   Check word counts for proposals (60-110