stems are compliant by design. Rather than viewing this as a bureaucratic burden, we should treat traceability as a technical challenge that enhances the reproducibility and scientific validity of our work. By standardizing these records, we can better analyze failure modes and accelerate the iterative refinement of our models.\n    *   *Number:* 15 (It's an operational change, not a fundamental shift in research goals).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Engineering.* Focus on building a standardized logging library.\n        *   Title: Automated Traceability Framework for High-Risk Models\n        *   What: A modular software library that automatically captures timestamps and input data as required by Article 12. It integrates directly into the model's inference engine to ensure seamless event recording.\n        *   How: We will implement middleware that hooks into API calls to log metadata and reference database queries in a tamper-proof ledger. This will be integrated into our existing CI/CD pipeline for all high-risk projects.\n        *   Why: This ensures regulatory compliance without manually altering every individual experiment's code. (Word count check: ~80 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Scientific Research on Interpretability.* Focus on making logs useful for science, not just law.\n        *   Title: Semantic Logging for AI Behavioral Analysis\n        *   What: A research project to develop \"semantic logs\" that record internal model states alongside the required regulatory events. This moves beyond simple event logging toward a comprehensive map of system decision-making.\n        *   How: We will design new telemetry probes that capture activation patterns and attention weights during high-risk triggers. These data streams will be correlated with the mandatory Article 12 timestamps for deep forensic analysis.\n        *   Why: It transforms a compliance requirement into a powerful tool for scientific discovery and model debugging. (Word count c