get.\n    *   Infrastructure reuse is key.\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* We cannot ignore this, as market access depends on it, but we must avoid an overly prescriptive \"compliance-first\" culture that kills R&D agility. The focus should be on creating standardized, modular logging infrastructure that can be plugged into various projects rather than rebuilding every system from scratch. By treating these requirements as shared utilities, we maintain our scientific ambition while ensuring legal viability.\n    *   *Stance:* Moderate adjustment. Not a total overhaul (100), but more than business as usual (0). Maybe 40-60 range. Let's go with 45. It's a significant operational change, but not a change in *what* we are trying to achieve scientifically.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Shared Infrastructure/Middleware.* Instead of each project writing its own logs, create a centralized logging service.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Middleware\n        *   What: A standardized software layer that automatically captures the required telemetry and event logs for any high-risk AI pipeline.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop an API-based utility that researchers can integrate into their models to track usage times, inputs, and database references.\n        *   Why: This prevents redundant development work across different research teams and optimizes infrastructure costs.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Documentation Automation.* Technical documentation is tedious. Automate it.\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: A tool that extracts metadata from training scripts, dataset manifests, and validation reports to generate the required regulatory dossiers.\n        *   How: We will implement a version-control integrated system that auto-populates templates whenever a model is promoted to the testing phase.\n        *   Why: It reduces the administrative burden on scientists, allowing them to focus on R&D rather than manual record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Resource Audit/Budget Shift.* Need to allocate funds specifically for this.\n        *   Title: Compliance Infrastructure Funding Reallocation\n        *   What: A strategic shift in the annual budget to dedicate a specific percentage of compute and manpower to regulatory engineering.\n        *   How: We will identify low-priority legacy projects to sunset, redirecting those resources toward building the traceability frameworks required by Article 12.\n        *   Why: It ensures that compliance does not cannibalize the budgets of our most ambitious scientific pursuits.\n\n    *   Reasoning: 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Stance: 0-100 number? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Proposals distinct? (Middleware vs. Documentation Tool vs. Budget/Management). Yes.\n    *   Proposal format: title, what, how, why. Yes.\n    *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words. No bullet lists inside fields. Plain prose.\n    *   Role maintained? Lab Director focused on efficiency and resources.",
