odel performance metrics with the static requirements of the EU AI Act. This replaces stagnant PDF reports with active data streams.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement a version-controlled documentation pipeline using Git-based tools that link specific code changes directly to compliance reports and risk management logs.\",\n      \"why\": \"It ensures our technical dossiers remain current throughout the system lifetime as strictly mandated by Recital 71.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. Focus: knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning on how the lab should respond (3-5 sentences).\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) indicating how much the R&D agenda should change.\n        *   (3) Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals/projects.\n        *   For each proposal: Title (<10 words), What (1-2 sentences), How (1-2 sentences), Why (1 sentence).\n        *   Constraint: Each proposal must be between 60 and 110 words total. Plain prose only (no bullet points inside fields).\n\n    *   Article 12 focuses on *automatic recording of events (logs)* for high-risk AI systems to ensure traceability, risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational oversight.\n    *   Recital 71 emphasizes the need for comprehensible information on development, performance, algorithms, data, training, and validation.\n    *   Specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) (biometric ID/etc.) include timestamps, reference databases, input matches, and human verifier IDs.\n\n    *   As a researcher focused on the \"knowledge frontier,\" I see this as a shift from \"black-box\" experimentation to \"auditable\" development.\n    *   It's not just about compliance; it's an opportunity to advance *interpretability* and *reproducibility*.\n    *   Response: We need to integrate logging into the architectural design of our systems rather than treating it as a post-hoc wrapper. This ensures that scientific rigor is maintained alongside regulatory compliance.\n\n    *   Does this change *what* we research (the goals)? Not fundamentally.\n    *   Does it change *how* we research (the methodology)? Yes.\n    *   It doesn't require a total overhaul of the \"intellectual merit\" or \"scientific impact,\" but it necessitates a shift in infrastructure and documentation standards.\n    *   Score: 20-30. It's an operational change more than a strategic pivot. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling.* Creating a standardized logging framework for the lab to ensure all high-risk projects are automatically compliant without hindering research speed.\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Logging Framework (ACLF)\n        *   What: A universal middleware layer that captures system events, input-output pairs, and versioning metadata across different AI architectures.\n        *   How: We will develop a standardized API integrated into our training pipelines to automatically generate audit trails.\n        *   Why: It reduces the manual burden on researchers while ensuring every experiment is traceable for regulatory audits.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Theoretical Research/Interpretability.* Moving from simple logs (who did what) to semantic logs (why it happened). This aligns with \"intellectual merit.\"\n        *   Title: Semantic Traceability and Interpretability Research\n        *   What: A research project to develop \"interpretable logs\" that translate raw system events into human-understandable reasoning chains.\n        *   How: We will apply Large Language Model probing techniques to map internal state changes to the event logs required by Article 12.\n        *   Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution toward AI transparency and explainability.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Governance/Documentation Standards.* Developing new protocols for technical documentation as requested in Recital 71.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Protocol\n        *   What: A living documentation system that synchronizes real-time model performance metrics with the static requirements of the AI Act.\n        *   How: We will implement a version-controlled documentation pipeline using Git-based tools that link code changes directly to compliance reports.\n        *   Why: It ensures our technical dossiers remain up-to-date throughout the system lifetime as mandated by the regulation.\n\n    *   *Constraint Check:* 60-110 words per proposal? Plain prose (no bullets)? Title < 10 words?\n    *   Proposal 1: ~65 words.\n    *   Proposal 2: ~70 words.\n    *   Proposal 3: ~70 words.\n\n    *Check Word Counts:*\n    P1:\n    Title: Automated Compliance Logging Framework (4)\n    What: A universal middleware layer that captures system events, input-output pairs, and versioning metadata across different AI architectures. (18)\n    How: We will develop a standardized API integrated into our training pipelines to automatically generate audit trails for all high-risk models. (20)\n    Why: It reduces the manual burden on researchers while ensuring every experiment is traceable for regulatory audits. (16)\n    Total: ~58 words. *Need to expand slightly to hit 60.*\n\n    *Revised P1:*\n    Title: Automated Compliance Logging Framework\n    What: A universal middleware layer that captures system events, input-output pairs, and versioning metadata across various AI architectures. (19)\n    How: We will develop a standardized API integrated into our training pipelines to automatically generate comprehensive audit trails for all high-risk models. (21)\n    Why: It reduces the manual burden on researchers while ensuring every single experiment is fully traceable for regulatory audits. (18)\n    Total: ~63 words. Good.\n\n    P2:\n    Title: Semantic Traceability and Interpretability Research\n    What: A research project to develop interpretable logs that translate raw system events into human-understandable reasoning chains. (17)\n    How: We will apply advanced probing techniques to map internal state changes of the AI to the event logs required by Article 12. (21)\n    Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution toward global AI transparency and explainability standards. (17)\n    Total: ~60 words. Good.\n\n    P3:\n    Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Protocol\n    What: A living documentation system that synchronizes real-time model performance metrics with the static requirements of the EU AI Act. (19)\n    How: We will implement a version-controlled documentation pipeline using Git-based tools that link specific code changes directly to compliance reports. (20)\n    Why: It ensures our technical dossiers remain current throughout the system lifetime as strictly mandated by Recital 71. (18)\n    Total: ~62 words. Good.\n\n    *   Researcher role? Yes.\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Number 0-100? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/concrete? Yes (Tooling, Research, Governance).\n    *   Fields: title, what, how, why? Yes.\n    *   Word counts (60-110)? Yes.\n    *   Plain prose (no bullets inside)? Yes.",
