ck: ~70 words. OK.\n    *   Proposal 3 check: ~75 words. OK.\n    *   Check \"no bullet lists inside fields\". Use plain prose.\n    *   Role check: Development Engineer focusing on viability and speed.\n\n    *Self-Correction during drafting:* I need to make sure the reasoning isn't too high (e.g., 80) because we aren't changing the *AI research*, just adding engineering requirements. But it can't be 0 because \"business as usual\" is illegal now. 30 feels right—it's a significant addition to the workload but doesn't stop us from doing AI.\n\n    *Drafting the proposals carefully:*\n    Proposal 1:\n    Title: Unified Traceability and Telemetry Wrapper\n    What: This is a standardized software library that automatically captures system events, timestamps, and input-output pairs across all high-risk prototypes.\n    How: We will implement a modular middleware layer using an asynchronous logging pipeline to ensure telemetry doesn't degrade inference latency during testing.\n    Why: It prevents researchers from reinventing the wheel, maintaining prototyping speed while guaranteeing regulatory compliance across diverse projects. (74 words)\n\n    Proposal 2:\n    Title: Automated Dataset and Reference Mapping Tool\n    What: This tool will track which specific version of a reference database was used for every single query processed by high-risk AI systems.\n    How: We will integrate data versioning controls with our inference engine to timestamp and link each request to a unique dataset hash in the logs.\n    Why: This directly satisfies the Article 12 requirement regarding the identification of reference databases used during system checks. (73 words)\n\n    Proposal 3:\n    Title: Verification Log Capture Interface\n    What: We will create a dedicated UI module for human reviewers to sign off on AI results, capturing their identity and timestamps automatically.\n    How: The lab will build a verification gateway that requires an authenticated user ID before any 