oals remain valid, our engineering standards must now treat \"traceability\" as a first-class requirement rather than an afterthought.\n    *   *Number:* 25 (The *agenda* of what we are researching doesn't change much, but the *process* and some *infrastructure projects* do. It's not a total overhaul of R&D goals, but it is a significant operational shift). Wait, maybe 30? Let's go with 30 because implementing these logs into existing legacy models might require architectural changes.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* Build a centralized logging middleware.\n        *   Title: Unified Regulatory Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library that automatically captures and stores required event logs across all high-risk projects. It ensures consistent timestamps and data provenance records.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop an API wrapper for our AI modules to pipe events into a secure, immutable ledger. This avoids manual logging by individual researchers.\n        *   Why: This guarantees institutional compliance and prevents human error in record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Documentation.* Technical documentation overhaul.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An automated system that links model versioning, training data sets, and validation results into a live compliance dossier. It replaces static PDF reports with a queryable database.\n        *   How: We will integrate our Git repositories and MLflow experiments with a documentation generator tailored to the EU AI Act requirements. This creates an audit trail from data ingestion to deployment.\n        *   Why: It minimizes the legal risk associated with outdated or incomplete technical documentation.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specific Annex III focus (The \"Human-in-the-loop\" tracking).* Tracking natural persons involved in verification.\n        *   Title: Human Verifier Attribution System\n        