tamps every manual verification with a verified user ID and timestamp.\",\n      \"why\": \"This ensures the lab meets the specific mandate for identifying natural persons involved in the verification of high-risk system results.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution.\nTechnical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act, Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71. Focus on high-risk AI systems, automatic recording of events (logs), traceability, and specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a).\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  Stance score (0-100) on agenda change.\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals (Title, What, How, Why).\n            *   Each proposal must be 60-110 words total.\n            *   Plain prose only (no bullet lists inside fields).\n            *   Stay in role.\n\n    *   Article 12 requires high-risk AI systems to have automatic logging capabilities for the lifetime of the system.\n    *   Logs must help identify risks, facilitate post-market monitoring, and monitor operation.\n    *   Annex III point 1(a) has very specific requirements (timestamps, database refs, input data, human verifiers).\n    *   Recital 71 emphasizes technical documentation (algorithms, data, training, testing).\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer focused on prototyping speed and viability, I see this as an overhead. If we don't integrate logging into our core infrastructure *now*, every prototype will need expensive retrofitting later to be compliant. We shouldn't pivot the entire research agenda (we aren't becoming a compliance firm), but we must shift *how* we build systems.\n\n    *   Reasoning: The lab needs to integrate standardized, automated logging frameworks into our core development pipeline to avoid costly retrofits for high-risk prototypes. While this adds overhead, automating these requirements ensures that technical viability is maintained without sacrificing prototyping speed. We should prioritize creating modular \"compliance wrappers\" that can be applied across different AI projects consistently.\n\n    *   Score: 30 (It's a change in *process/implementation*, not necessarily a change in the *research goals* or what we are trying to invent).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Standardization.* Create a shared logging library.\n        *   Title: Unified Logging Framework for High-Risk Systems\n        *   What: A standardized internal software library that automatically captures timestamps, input data, and system events across all prototypes.\n        *   How: We will develop an API wrapper based on OpenTelemetry to ensure consistent log formats regardless of the specific model architecture.\n        *   Why: This reduces the time engineers spend on manual record-keeping for each new project.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Data Governance/Provenance.* A system to track databases used.\n        *   Title: Automated Dataset Provenance and Versioning System\n        *   What: An automated registry that links specific model inferences back to the exact version of the reference database used during execution.\n        *   How: We will integrate DVC or MLflow with our runtime environment to log a cryptographic hash of the dataset for every request.\n        *   Why: It directly fulfills the Article 12 requirement regarding reference database traceability.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Human-in-the-loop (HITL) auditing tool.* Specifically for Annex III point 1(a).\n        *   Title: Verification Audit Interface for Natural Person Identification\n        *   What: A lightweight UI layer that forces and records the identity of the human verifier when reviewing high-risk AI outputs.\n        *   How: We will implement a secure authentication gate in our validation tools that stamps every manual verification with a user ID and timestamp.\n        *   Why: This ensures we meet the specific legal mandate for identifying natural persons involved in verification.\n\n    *   *Reasoning (3-5 sentences):* Check.\n    *   *Score (0-100):* Check.\n    *   *Exactly 3 proposals:* Check.\n    *   *Distinct/Concrete:* Yes (Infrastructure, Data Provenance, UI/HITL).\n