erify compliance at any point in the lifetime.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Governance/Audit (Internal Compliance Audits)*\n        *   Title: High-Risk System Pre-Deployment Audit Protocol\n        *   What: A formal internal review process that verifies a system's logging and record-keeping capabilities before it leaves the lab.\n        *   How: The compliance office will establish a checklist based on Article 12, requiring lead researchers to demonstrate log functionality for specific risk scenarios. We will conduct simulated audits to stress-test our ability to retrieve required event data.\n        *   Why: It shields the institution from regulatory penalties by catching omissions before they become legal liabilities.\n\n    *   Proposal 1 length check:\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Logging Architecture (4 words)\n        *   What: A standardized middleware layer that automatically captures and stores system events, timestamps, and input/output data across all high-risk projects. (21 words)\n        *   How: We will develop a centralized logging API that R&D teams must integrate into their model pipelines to ensure uniform data collection. This ensures we meet the automatic recording requirement without relying on manual developer effort. (34 words)\n        *   Why: It guarantees technical compliance and prevents costly retrospective engineering during audits. (12 words)\n        *   Total: ~71 words. Good.\n\n    *   Proposal 2 length check:\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation System (4 words)\n        *   What: A digital repository for living technical documentation that tracks the evolution of algorithms, datasets, and validation processes in real-time. (20 words)\n        *   How: We will implement a version-controlled documentation tool linked to our code commits and data registries to automatically update system characteristics. This transforms static documents into traceable records as required by Recital 71. (32 words)\n        *