ity itself. A score around 40-50 suggests a moderate adjustment—not changing *what* we solve, but adding *how* we track it to our research goals. Let's go with 40: an adjustment that elevates \"observability\" to a first-class research citizen.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (Technical/Infrastructure):* Automated Observability Framework. Create a tool that handles the logs automatically so researchers don't have to do it manually, but allows them to query the data for scientific insights.\n    *   *Proposal 2 (Methodological/Theoretical):* Research on \"Traceable AI.\" Study how different logging granularities affect our ability to detect model drift or bias in high-risk settings. This turns compliance into a paper/publication.\n    *   *Proposal 3 (Governance/Standardization):* Open-source Standard for High-Risk Logging. Lead the industry in defining what \"appropriate traceability\" looks like, ensuring we shape the standard rather than just following it.\n\n    *   *Constraint Check:* No bullet lists inside fields. Plain prose. Length limits (60-110 words total per proposal). 3 distinct things. Role: Researcher.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 refined:*\n        *   Title: Unified Observability Infrastructure\n        *   What: A centralized logging layer that automatically captures the events required by Article 12 across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will build a middleware API that intercepts system inputs and outputs to store timestamps and reference data in a queryable database.\n        *   Why: It removes administrative friction while providing researchers with rich datasets for analyzing model behavior in the wild.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 refined:*\n        *   Title: Empirical Study of Traceability Granularity\n        *   What: A research project to determine the optimal level of logging detail needed to identify systemic risks without degrading performance.\n        *   How: We will conduct ablation studies on different logging frequencies and data types 