omentum while ensuring that our high-risk outputs are rigorously verifiable. We must integrate these logging requirements into the experimental design phase rather than treating them as post-hoc administrative tasks.\n    *   Stance Score: 15/100. (I don't want a \"major overhaul\" of *what* we study, but I want a slight shift in *how* we document it to turn compliance into research merit).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Interpretability/Research Focus.* Turn logging into a data source for studying AI behavior.\n        *   Title: Automated Provenance Framework for Model Forensics.\n        *   What: A system that uses Article 12 logs to create detailed causal maps of decision-making processes in high-risk models.\n        *   How: We will develop an internal tool that correlates event logs with latent space activations during inference. This will allow researchers to backtrack from a risk-event to the specific training data or input trigger.\n        *   Why: It converts mandatory record-keeping into a powerful diagnostic tool for scientific discovery.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Engineering/Infrastructure (but Researchy).* Make logging \"zero-friction\" so it doesn't kill agility.\n        *   Title: Transparent Logging Middleware for Agile Experimentation.\n        *   What: A lightweight, non-intrusive API layer that automatically captures the required EU AI Act metadata without manual researcher input.\n        *   How: We will implement a wrapper around our core training and inference libraries to intercept inputs, outputs, and timestamps. This middleware will write directly to a secure, immutable ledger in the background.\n        *   Why: It protects the intellectual merit of research by removing the administrative burden from the scientists.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Theoretical/Methodological.* New benchmarks for \"Traceable AI.\"\n        *   Title: Benchmarking Traceability Metrics for High-Risk AI Systems.\n        *   What: A theoretical project to define and quant