at we research (e.g., switching from LLMs to something else). Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Standardized Logging Framework.* Create a library that every project uses so developers don't have to reinvent logging for each prototype.\n    *   *Idea 2: Automated Documentation Pipeline (MLOps).* Link the training pipeline directly to the technical documentation required by Recital 71.\n    *   *Idea 3: High-Risk Compliance Audit Tool.* A tool that checks if a system meets Article 12(3) requirements before it moves from prototype to deployment.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (Standardized Logging):*\n        *   Title: Unified Telemetry Wrapper for High-Risk Prototypes\n        *   What: A standardized software library providing consistent event logging interfaces across all lab projects. It ensures that essential metadata, such as timestamps and input references, are captured by default.\n        *   How: We will develop a Python decorator or middleware layer that wraps model inference calls to automatically record inputs, outputs, and execution times to a secure database. This avoids manual log implementation in every new prototype.\n        *   Why: It maintains prototyping speed while ensuring technical viability for regulatory compliance.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 (Automated Documentation):*\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An integrated pipeline that automatically exports training hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results into a compliant technical dossier. This addresses the Recital 71 requirement for up-to-date documentation throughout the system lifetime.\n        *   How: We will integrate MLflow or Weights & Biases with a custom reporting tool that generates human-readable PDF summaries of every model version. These reports will be linked to the specific code commit and dataset hash used.\n        *   Why: It removes the administrative burden from engineers, keeping them focused on R&D quality.\n\n  